/* generated by tools/make-lessons.js from tools/lessons/embeddings/*.js; do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = Object.assign(window.LESSONS || {}, {"embeddings/swiss-roll":{"idea":"A Swiss roll is a sheet curled in 3-D, so points far apart on the sheet can be close in 3-D. PCA, a straight projection, piles the layers on top of each other. t-SNE and UMAP trust only short distances, so they can unroll it.","why":"Real data often lies on a thin curved surface inside a space with many features. If your 2-D picture lays different parts of that surface on top of each other, you see neighbours that do not exist and you report groups or relations that are not there. You need a map you can trust locally, and a number that tests that trust.","analogy":"A rolled-up carpet. Photograph it from the end and you see the spiral of cloth, but the whole length of the carpet is squashed into one spot. Photograph it from the side and the layers hide each other. Neither picture shows the carpet as it is. To see the pattern you must unroll it. An ant on the carpet always knows which patch is next to which, because it walks on the cloth, not through the air between layers.","vocab":[["Manifold","The thin curved surface (or curve) your data lies on, even though it sits in a space with more dimensions. The roll is a 2-D sheet inside 3-D."],["Linear map (PCA)","Projects every point onto the same flat plane, the one along which the points spread most. It can tilt and squash, but it cannot unroll."],["Nonlinear embedding","A 2-D map built from neighbour relations (t-SNE, UMAP). Because it only keeps who is near whom, it can bend a sheet flat."],["Neighbourhood","The k points nearest to a given point. Here k = 10 when we score a map."],["Distance along the sheet","How far you must travel without leaving the surface. For two points on neighbouring turns of the roll it is much more than the straight line through the air between them."],["False neighbour","A point that is among the 10 nearest in the 2-D map but was not among the 10 nearest in the original 3-D data."],["Trustworthiness","A score from 0 to 1. For every point, look at its 10 nearest neighbours in the map and penalise each false neighbour by how far down the 3-D neighbour list it really was. 1 means no false neighbours."]],"steps":[{"h":"The data: a sheet curled up","p":"Each of the 350 points has three numbers (x, y, z), but only two numbers really matter: where the point is along the sheet (the colour) and its height y. A sheet has 2 degrees of freedom. The roll only hides that by bending it through 3-D."},{"h":"Straight-line distance can lie","p":"Take two points at the same height, one full turn apart along the sheet. In 3-D they are 6.3 apart. To walk from one to the other along the sheet you must travel 57. For nearby points the two distances agree. The further apart the points, the more they disagree."},{"h":"PCA can only take a shadow","p":"PCA keeps the two straight directions with the most spread and drops the third. A roll spreads almost equally in all three (about 42%, 30% and 28% of the variance), so PCA has no good plane to choose. Drop the height and you get a spiral with every height piled on one spot. Drop another direction and the layers overlap."},{"h":"Use only short distances","p":"t-SNE and UMAP first record, for each point, who its close neighbours are (about 30 for perplexity 30, 15 for UMAP). In a small patch the sheet is almost flat, so straight distance is honest there. They then place the points in 2-D so that those neighbour relations hold."},{"h":"The price: cuts and shuffled pieces","p":"Knowing only local neighbours, the method is free to cut the sheet and put the pieces anywhere, flipped or turned. You often see separate strands in the picture. Inside a strand the colours change smoothly. Where the strands sit relative to each other carries no meaning."},{"h":"Measure the trust","p":"For each point take its 10 nearest in the map. Any that were not among its 10 nearest in 3-D are false neighbours, penalised by (3-D rank minus 10). Add up and rescale to 0 to 1. On a 350-point roll: in PCA's map 45% of neighbours are false and they are on average the 94th nearest in 3-D (score 0.89). In t-SNE's map about 16% are false, but typically only the 17th nearest, a near miss (score 0.997)."},{"h":"What trustworthiness cannot see","p":"It looks for false neighbours only. A map that cuts the sheet into a dozen strands still scores 0.99, because every strand keeps its true neighbours. And it is judged against the 3-D points you gave it: if noise pushes layers into each other, those wrong neighbours count as right. Always read the score together with the colours."}],"example":{"title":"two points 6.3 apart that are 57 apart on the sheet","setup":"The widget's roll has x = t·cos t, z = t·sin t, with t between 4.7 and 14.1, and y between 0 and 21. Seen from the end it is a spiral whose radius equals t. Pick the point at <b>t = 6.0</b> and compare it with three others at the same height.","table":{"head":["Second point","Straight line in 3-D","Along the sheet ≈ (t₂² − t₁²)/2","Along ÷ straight"],"rows":[["t = 6.2 (a short step)","1.23","1.2","1.0"],["t = 6.0 + π (half a turn)","15.14","23.8","1.6"],["t = 6.0 + 2π (one full turn)","6.28","57.4","9.1"]]},"walk":["First point: x = 6.0·cos 6.0 = 5.76 and z = 6.0·sin 6.0 = −1.68 (angles in radians).","One full turn later, t = 6.0 + 2π = 12.28. The angle is the same, so the point lies on the same ray out from the axis: x = 12.28·cos 6.0 = 11.79 and z = −3.43.","Straight line (same y, so ignore it): √((11.79 − 5.76)² + (−3.43 + 1.68)²) = √39.5 = <b>6.28</b>. Both points are on one ray, so this is just the difference of the radii, 12.28 − 6.00 = 2π. The gap between layers is 2π everywhere on the roll.","Along the sheet: a small step dt moves you about t·dt along the spiral (the radius is t). So the length from t₁ to t₂ is about ∫ t dt = (t₂² − t₁²)/2 = (150.88 − 36)/2 = <b>57.4</b>. The exact integral, which keeps the small extra √(1 + t²), gives 57.8.","Ratio: 57.4 / 6.28 = 9.1. Believe the straight line and these two points look like neighbours. On the sheet they are nine times further apart.","Now the safe zone. With 350 points the median distance from a point to its 10th nearest neighbour is 4.2, which is less than the layer gap 6.3, so a 10-neighbour list stays inside one layer. With 150 points it is 6.4 and the lists reach the next layer. With 600 points it is 3.2."],"result":"A neighbourhood is trustworthy while it is smaller than the gap between layers (6.3). At 350 points and noise 0.3 only 1.5% of the entries in the 10-neighbour lists come from a part of the sheet more than half a turn away. At noise 2.0 it is 13.8%, and the maps copy those wrong neighbours."},"read":[["The Swiss roll in 3-D (top)","Your points, coloured by position along the sheet (light to dark). Drag to rotate."],["Button: Look along the roll's axis","Shows the spiral cross-section. The colours run in order, but the whole height of the roll is squashed onto the spiral."],["Button: Side view","Shows a rectangle: the layers sit behind one another and hide each other."],["PCA card","A flat straight-line shadow of the roll. Look for colour bands that cross or pile up."],["t-SNE card and UMAP card","Unrolled maps. Colours should change smoothly along each strand. Strands may be cut, shuffled and flipped."],["Caption: trustworthiness 0.xxx","The score from step 6 for that map, also listed in the panel <i>Trustworthiness (10 neighbours)</i>. Higher is better, 1 is perfect. It is judged against the 3-D points you see, noise included, and it catches false neighbours, not cuts. It shows \"running…\" while the map is still settling."],["Readout: Variance PCA keeps","The share of the roll's spread inside the two PCA directions, about 70 to 74%. The missing 26 to 30% is the direction PCA threw away."],["Slider: Points and Noise","How many points, and how much random jitter is added to x, y and z. Noise makes the layers blur into each other."],["Sliders: t-SNE perplexity and UMAP n_neighbors","How many neighbours each method looks at. Small gives short strands, large gives big sweeping shapes."],["Button: Re-run with a new seed","Draws a new roll (new points) and restarts both methods, so every score can change."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the poll. Then press <i>Look along the roll's axis</i>, <i>Side view</i> and <i>Default view</i>, and compare each with the <i>PCA</i> card. Finally press <i>Re-run with a new seed</i> a few times and watch the PCA card.","see":"The axis view is a clean spiral, the side view a rectangle with hidden layers. At the default data the PCA card is a tilted shadow between the two, with colour bands crossing. On most new seeds (4 of the next 5 we tried) the PCA card turns into a near copy of the spiral, yet its score stays only 0.92 to 0.94.","why":"PCA keeps the two directions of largest spread. A roll has three almost equal ones, so which one it drops depends on the sample. Every straight 2-D view loses a whole direction: a spiral hides the height, and then points 21 units apart vertically become map neighbours."},{"do":"Move <i>Noise</i> from 0.30 up to 2.00 and watch the t-SNE and UMAP cards and their scores.","see":"The colour gradients break up into patches. The scores slip only a little: t-SNE from about 0.997 to 0.992 and UMAP from 0.993 to 0.986, while PCA stays near 0.88.","why":"Noise pushes layers into each other: 1.5% of 10-neighbour entries come from a part of the sheet more than half a turn away at noise 0.3, and 13.8% at noise 2.0. The maps copy those neighbours, and the score is measured against the same noisy 3-D points, so it hardly notices."},{"do":"Set <i>t-SNE perplexity</i> to 5 and <i>UMAP n_neighbors</i> to 5.","see":"Both maps break into many short strands, a dozen or more. Scores stay high: about 0.993 for t-SNE and 0.983 for UMAP.","why":"Tiny neighbourhoods have too little overlap to stitch the strands together, and trustworthiness does not penalise cuts, only false neighbours."},{"do":"Return to the defaults and set <i>Points</i> to 150, then to 600.","see":"With 150 points both scores fall to about 0.98. With 600 they rise to about 0.999 (t-SNE) and 0.997 (UMAP).","why":"With fewer points each neighbourhood is wider: the 10th neighbour is 6.4 away at 150 points, 3.2 at 600, against a layer gap of 6.3. Once the neighbourhood reaches the next layer, unrolling gets harder."},{"do":"Press <i>Re-run with a new seed</i> five times and write down the three scores each time.","see":"PCA wanders between about 0.87 and 0.94. t-SNE stays within 0.996 to 0.998, UMAP within 0.992 to 0.995.","why":"PCA's plane depends on which sample you drew, since its three spreads are nearly tied. The neighbour-based methods do not depend on any plane."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"An <b>ant on the carpet</b>. Neighbours should be the places the ant reaches in a few steps, not what a bird sees across the layers."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Straight-line distance is trustworthy only <b>inside a small patch</b>. Keep the neighbourhood smaller than the gap between layers (6.3 here: the 10th neighbour was 4.2 away at 350 points)."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Colour the map by something you know (here the position t). Smooth colour change along a strand means it is unrolled. Crossing bands mean it is flattened or glued."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Trustworthiness = 1 minus (a penalty for every false neighbour, bigger the further down the 3-D list it was). It counts <b>intruders</b>, not cuts."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"In an unrolled map only <b>who is next to whom</b> is real. Flips, turns, gaps and the order of the strands are free choices of the method."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"PCA is just a worse t-SNE.\"","right":"PCA answers a different question: which straight directions carry the most spread. For data on a flat or nearly flat surface it is excellent, fast and repeatable, and it can place new points. On a rolled sheet the straight-direction answer is a flat shadow."},{"wrong":"\"A trustworthiness of 0.99 means the sheet is unrolled correctly.\"","right":"It only says that map neighbours were also 3-D neighbours. A map cut into a dozen strands can still score 0.99, and with noisy data it is judged against noisy neighbours. Check the colours as well."},{"wrong":"\"The distance between two strands in the t-SNE or UMAP map tells me how far apart those parts of the sheet are.\"","right":"Strands can be placed, flipped or turned anywhere. Only the neighbours inside a strand are reliable."},{"wrong":"\"A bigger perplexity or n_neighbors is always safer.\"","right":"Past the layer gap a neighbourhood reaches the next turn and colours begin to mix. Perplexity 80 with n_neighbors 60 gave about 0.989 and 0.986 here, against 0.997 and 0.993 at the defaults."}],"code":[{"title":"The straight line against the walk along the sheet","py":"import numpy as np\n\nt = np.array([6.0, 6.0 + 2 * np.pi])        # sheet positions: a point, and the same spot one turn further out\nxz = np.c_[t * np.cos(t), t * np.sin(t)]    # the roll's x and z coordinates (same height y, so y cancels)\n\nstraight = np.linalg.norm(xz[1] - xz[0])    # straight-line distance in 3-D\nalong = (t[1] ** 2 - t[0] ** 2) / 2         # length of sheet between them: the spiral r = t\nprint(\"straight line:\", round(straight, 2))\nprint(\"along the sheet:\", round(along, 1))\nprint(\"ratio:\", round(along / straight, 1))","out":"straight line: 6.28\nalong the sheet: 57.4\nratio: 9.1","walk":[["t = np.array([6.0, 6.0 + 2 * np.pi])","Two positions along the sheet: a point and the point one full turn further. <code>2 * np.pi</code> is one full circle in radians."],["np.c_[t * np.cos(t), t * np.sin(t)]","The roll's formula x = t·cos t and z = t·sin t. <code>np.c_</code> puts the two columns side by side, giving a 2 by 2 array: one row per point. Height y is left out because both points share it."],["np.linalg.norm(xz[1] - xz[0])","Subtract the two rows to get the difference vector, then <code>norm</code> gives its length: the straight-line distance."],["(t[1] ** 2 - t[0] ** 2) / 2","The length of the spiral between the two points, from ∫ t dt. <code>**</code> means \"to the power of\". It is a good approximation when t is above 4."],["round(x, 2)","Print only 2 decimals so the output is short and the same on every machine."]],"tip":"Whenever a method claims \"distance\", ask: along what path? Straight lines through empty space are not always allowed."},{"title":"PCA on the roll: how the spread splits, and its trustworthiness","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_swiss_roll\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import PCA\nfrom sklearn.manifold import trustworthiness\n\nX, t = make_swiss_roll(n_samples=350, noise=0.3, random_state=0)\npca = PCA(n_components=3).fit(X)             # keep all 3 to see every variance share\nprint(\"variance shares:\", pca.explained_variance_ratio_.round(2))\n\nE = PCA(n_components=2).fit_transform(X)     # the 2-D PCA map\nprint(\"PCA map trust:\", round(trustworthiness(X, E, n_neighbors=10), 2))","out":"variance shares: [0.42 0.3  0.28]\nPCA map trust: 0.89","walk":[["make_swiss_roll(n_samples=350, noise=0.3, random_state=0)","scikit-learn's generator, with the same formula as the simulation. It returns <code>X</code> (350 rows, 3 columns) and <code>t</code>, the position along the sheet. We never give <code>t</code> to PCA, t-SNE or UMAP. It is only for colouring. <code>random_state</code> fixes the random draw."],["PCA(n_components=3).fit(X)","Learn all three directions. <code>fit</code> learns from the data and changes the object."],["pca.explained_variance_ratio_","A learned result (names ending in an underscore are filled in by <code>fit</code>). The share of the total spread along each direction. The three add to 1. Here no direction is much bigger than the others, so no 2-D plane is a clear winner."],["PCA(n_components=2).fit_transform(X)","<code>fit_transform</code> is <code>fit</code> followed by <code>transform</code>: learn the plane, then project the points onto it. The result has shape (350, 2)."],["trustworthiness(X, E, n_neighbors=10)","Compares each point's 10 nearest neighbours in the map <code>E</code> against the original <code>X</code>. First argument original, second the map."]],"tip":"Pass the original data first and the map second. Swapping them gives a different (wrong) score."},{"title":"t-SNE and UMAP on the same roll","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_swiss_roll\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE, trustworthiness\nimport umap\n\nX, t = make_swiss_roll(n_samples=350, noise=0.3, random_state=0)\ntsne = TSNE(perplexity=30, init=\"pca\", random_state=0).fit_transform(X)\num = umap.UMAP(n_neighbors=15, random_state=0).fit_transform(X)\n\nfor name, E in [(\"t-SNE\", tsne), (\"UMAP\", um)]:\n    print(name, \"trust above 0.98:\", trustworthiness(X, E, n_neighbors=10) > 0.98)\nprint(\"map shape:\", um.shape)","out":"t-SNE trust above 0.98: True\nUMAP trust above 0.98: True\nmap shape: (350, 2)","walk":[["TSNE(perplexity=30, init=\"pca\", random_state=0)","<code>perplexity</code> is the neighbourhood size. <code>init=\"pca\"</code> starts the points at PCA positions, which makes the layout steadier. <code>random_state</code> fixes the remaining randomness."],[".fit_transform(X)","t-SNE learns and returns the map in one go. There is no separate <code>transform</code> for new points (see the <i>New points</i> lesson)."],["umap.UMAP(n_neighbors=15, random_state=0)","<code>n_neighbors</code> is UMAP's neighbourhood size. Setting <code>random_state</code> also makes UMAP run on one thread, so the result is repeatable."],["for name, E in [...]","Loop over (label, map) pairs so the same check is written once."],["trustworthiness(...) > 0.98","We print True or False rather than the number, because t-SNE and UMAP outputs differ a little between library versions. The conclusion, both far above PCA's 0.89, does not."],["um.shape","(350, 2): one row per point, two map coordinates per row."]],"tip":"Always set <code>random_state</code> for t-SNE and UMAP, or the picture changes every time you run the cell."}],"check":[{"q":"Why does PCA fail to unroll a Swiss roll?","options":["It needs class labels to work","It cannot handle data with 3 features","It is a straight projection, so parts of the sheet that are far apart along it land on top of each other","It is random, so each run gives a different picture"],"answer":2,"why":"PCA projects every point onto one flat plane. Points on different turns of the roll can be above one another along the projection direction, so they land together even though they are far apart on the sheet."},{"q":"For the point at t = 6.0, how far is the walk along the sheet to the same spot one full turn further out (t = 6.0 + 2π = 12.28)? Use (t₂² − t₁²)/2.","options":["6.3","57","150","23"],"answer":1,"why":"(12.28² − 6.0²)/2 = (150.8 − 36)/2 = 57.4. The straight line through the air is only 2π = 6.3, so a method that trusts long straight distances would call these points neighbours."},{"q":"You press <i>Re-run with a new seed</i> five times. Which of the three scores will change the most between presses?","options":["All change equally","PCA","t-SNE","UMAP"],"answer":1,"why":"PCA's best plane depends on the sample because the three variance shares are nearly tied. Its score wandered between about 0.87 and 0.94, while t-SNE stayed within 0.996 to 0.998 and UMAP within 0.992 to 0.995."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why PCA cannot unroll a Swiss roll but t-SNE and UMAP can, and what trustworthiness does and does not check.","model":"PCA is a straight projection, so it has to drop a direction of the roll and layers that are far apart along the sheet end up on top of each other. t-SNE and UMAP only keep each point's nearest neighbours, which are reliable in a small flat patch, so they can lay the sheet out, at the price of cuts. Trustworthiness checks that a point's neighbours in the map were really its neighbours in 3-D, so it catches false neighbours but not a torn sheet."},"cheat":"Data on a curved sheet cannot be flattened by a straight projection: PCA drops a direction and piles layers up. t-SNE and UMAP keep only local neighbour relations, which are safe while a neighbourhood is smaller than the gap between layers (6.3 here), so they unroll the sheet but may cut it and place pieces anywhere. Trustworthiness (10 neighbours) catches false neighbours, not cuts."},"embeddings/tsne-perplexity":{"idea":"t-SNE gives every point its own bell curve of neighbours. Perplexity is the dial that sets how wide each curve is: it means \"about this many neighbours count\". The 2-D map is then arranged to copy those neighbour weights.","why":"Points in dense regions have close neighbours, points in sparse regions have far ones. One fixed bell width would give dense points dozens of neighbours and sparse points almost none. Perplexity adapts the width per point. Set it badly and real groups split into fragments (too low) or different groups get glued together (too high).","analogy":"Ask each student for \"your 5 closest friends\". In a crowded hostel they live within a few metres. In a village they live kilometres away. \"Friends within 100 m\" would give a hostel student 200 friends and a villager none. \"About 5 friends, however far\" gives everyone a comparable circle. The count is perplexity. The radius it forces on each person is sigma (σ).","vocab":[["Perplexity","The effective number of neighbours of a point: e<sup>H</sup>, where H is the entropy of its neighbour weights. If 5 neighbours share the weight equally it is exactly 5."],["σ (sigma)","The width of a point's bell curve. Neighbour j gets weight exp(−d²/2σ²), where d is its distance. Each point gets its own σ."],["p(j|i)","The weight of point j in point i's neighbourhood, divided by the total, so the weights of one point add up to 1."],["Joint P","The symmetric version, P<sub>ij</sub> = (p(j|i) + p(i|j)) / 2n. Over all pairs it adds up to 1. This is the target the map must copy."],["Q (Student-t)","The same idea in the map: similarity 1/(1 + distance²), divided by the total over all pairs. The slow fall-off lets far-apart clusters sit apart without being crushed."],["KL divergence","Σ P<sub>ij</sub> log(P<sub>ij</sub>/Q<sub>ij</sub>): how badly the map's Q matches the target P. 0 is a perfect match. t-SNE moves the points to lower it."],["Neighbour purity","Of a point's 10 nearest neighbours in the map, the fraction that carry the same digit label. 1 means every neighbour is the same digit."]],"steps":[{"h":"The goal","p":"You have 300 digit images, each 64 numbers. You want to place each one on a 2-D page so that images that look alike stay close. First you need an exact score of \"looks alike\" for every pair of images. t-SNE builds that score from distances."},{"h":"A bell curve per point","p":"For point i, give every other point j the weight exp(−d²/2σ²), where d is the distance between i and j. A near point gets a weight close to 1, a far point close to 0. Divide by the total so the weights add to 1. These are the p(j|i). σ says how far the bell reaches."},{"h":"One σ for everyone fails","p":"On the left of the simulation there are three blobs: tight, medium and loose. With σ = 0.25, right for the tight blob, a typical point of the loose blob has an effective 2 neighbours, so only its nearest counts. With σ = 1.43, right for the loose blob, a tight-blob point sees about 64 neighbours, more than its whole blob."},{"h":"Perplexity: tune σ per point","p":"Entropy H = −Σ p log p measures how spread out a point's weights are. e<sup>H</sup> is the effective number of neighbours, called perplexity. t-SNE searches a separate σ for each point, by halving a bracket, until e<sup>H</sup> equals the number you chose. Dense points end with a narrow bell, sparse points with a wide one."},{"h":"Make it symmetric","p":"p(j|i) and p(i|j) differ: a point in a sparse region may list a dense-region point as a neighbour, but not the other way round. t-SNE averages the two, P<sub>ij</sub> = (p(j|i) + p(i|j))/2n. Now every point has a total pair weight of at least 1/2n, so even an outlier is pulled into the map."},{"h":"The map side","p":"In 2-D each point gets coordinates. Their similarity is Q<sub>ij</sub> ∝ 1/(1 + distance²). This curve falls slowly (a heavy tail). A cluster of 30 points needs room, and the heavy tail lets far clusters sit well apart without pushing the near ones together."},{"h":"Fit by KL","p":"Move the points downhill on KL(P‖Q). Two neighbours (big P) drawn far apart (small Q) cost a lot. Two non-neighbours (tiny P) drawn close cost little. So t-SNE guards neighbourhoods well and says little about where far clusters sit, which is the point of the <i>seeds</i> lesson."},{"h":"Why KL cannot choose the perplexity","p":"Each perplexity makes a different P, so each run is graded against a different target. KL usually falls as perplexity grows because a smoother target is easier to match, yet the map is not better. Use a yardstick that does not depend on P: trustworthiness, neighbour purity, and your eyes on several settings."}],"example":{"title":"one point's neighbourhood, with σ found by hand","setup":"A point i has four neighbours at distances <b>1, 2, 4 and 8</b>. You want perplexity 3, \"about three neighbours count\". Which σ does that need? Weight of a neighbour at distance d is exp(−d²/(2σ²)).","table":{"head":["σ","Weights for d = 1, 2, 4, 8","p(j|i)","Perplexity e<sup>H</sup>"],"rows":[["1","0.607, 0.135, 0.000, 0.000","0.817, 0.182, 0.000, 0.000","1.61 (too narrow)"],["2","0.883, 0.607, 0.135, 0.000","0.543, 0.373, 0.083, 0.000","2.48"],["3","0.946, 0.801, 0.411, 0.029","0.433, 0.366, 0.188, 0.013","3.01 (on target)"],["4","0.969, 0.883, 0.607, 0.135","0.374, 0.340, 0.234, 0.052","3.42 (too wide)"]]},"walk":["Start with σ = 1. Weights: exp(−1/2) = 0.607, exp(−4/2) = 0.135, exp(−16/2) = 0.0003, and about 0 for d = 8. They add to 0.742, so p = (0.817, 0.182, 0.000, 0.000). Almost everything sits on the nearest neighbour, and e<sup>H</sup> = 1.61. Too small: widen the bell.","Try σ = 4. Weights exp(−d²/32): 0.969, 0.883, 0.607, 0.135. Sum 2.594, so p = (0.374, 0.340, 0.234, 0.052), e<sup>H</sup> = 3.42. Too big. The most you can get from four neighbours is 4 (all weights equal), so 3.42 is already wide.","The right σ lies between 1 and 4. Halve the bracket: σ = 2.5 gives 2.77 (too small), σ = 3.25 gives 3.12 (too big), σ = 3 gives 3.008. This is the binary search the code runs, per point, until the entropy matches to 5 decimals.","Check σ = 3 by hand. Weights exp(−d²/18): 0.946, 0.801, 0.411, 0.029. Sum 2.186, so p = (0.433, 0.366, 0.188, 0.013).","Entropy H = −Σ p ln p = 0.433·0.838 + 0.366·1.004 + 0.188·1.671 + 0.013·4.338 = 0.363 + 0.368 + 0.314 + 0.057 = 1.10.","Perplexity = e<sup>1.10</sup> = 3.0. Done: σ ≈ 3 for this point. Stretch all four distances by 4 (a looser region) and the same recipe returns σ = 12 and the same p. Only the ratio d/σ matters."],"result":"σ = 3 gives perplexity 3.01: the nearest neighbour carries 43% of the weight, the farthest 1.3%. In the simulation at perplexity 30 a typical point of the tight blob has σ ≈ 0.24, of the medium blob 0.72 and of the loose blob 1.43, yet each one reads \"Perplexity achieved 30\"."},"read":[["Left plot: blue dots","140 points in three blobs of different spread (60 tight, 45 medium, 35 loose). Click a dot to choose the point whose neighbourhood you want to see (drawn as a star)."],["Dot size and fading","Size and opacity show the weight of each dot in the chosen point's neighbourhood. A big solid dot is a heavy neighbour, a tiny faint dot barely counts."],["Solid ring and dashed ring","The distance σ and 2σ from the chosen point. The label above the star prints σ."],["Readout: Gaussian width σ","The σ found for the chosen point at the current perplexity."],["Readout: Perplexity achieved","e<sup>H</sup> of the weights the search ended with. It should equal the target in brackets. The left panel stops at (140 − 1)/3 = 46, so for slider values above 46 it reads \"target 46\"."],["Readout: Points with ≥ 10% of the top weight","A head-count of dots that matter at least a tenth as much as the heaviest one. A rough, visible stand-in for perplexity."],["Right plot: t-SNE map of the digits","300 digit images (30 per class, 64 pixels each), written as their digit and coloured by class. Hover shows the class. The slider drives both plots."],["Readouts: KL divergence, Trustworthiness, Neighbour purity","Filled in when the run finishes (the bar shows progress). KL depends on the perplexity chosen. The other two are comparable across settings."],["Table: Runs so far","One row per perplexity you have run, sorted, newest run replacing an older one at the same perplexity. <i>Clear table</i> empties it."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Leave perplexity at 30. Click a dot in the left (tight) blob, read σ, then click a dot in the right (loose) blob.","see":"σ is about 0.25 for the tight blob and about 1.4 for the loose one (the medium blob gives about 0.7). Both read \"Perplexity achieved 30.0 (target 30)\", and 26 to 31 dots pass the 10% test in each case.","why":"The search widens the bell where points are far apart and narrows it where they are close, so every point ends with the same effective neighbour count. σ absorbs the difference in density."},{"do":"Drag perplexity down to 2 and look at the rings and at \"Points with ≥ 10% of the top weight\".","see":"The rings shrink to about the distance of the nearest neighbour (σ ≈ 0.05 for a tight-blob point, 0.3 for a loose-blob point) and only about 2 dots stay visible.","why":"Perplexity 2 means \"two neighbours count\". Each point pairs up with its nearest one or two, so the graph is nearly a set of tiny pairs."},{"do":"Drag perplexity up to 80 and watch the left readout.","see":"The left panel stops changing at 46 and the readout says \"target 46\" (a note on the page explains the limit). The digits map on the right does change.","why":"The left set has 140 points and the page caps perplexity at (n − 1)/3. The digits set has 300 points, so its cap is about 99."},{"do":"Run the digits at perplexity 2, 5, 15, 30 and 80 (wait for each run to finish) and compare the table.","see":"KL falls from about 0.45 at perplexity 2 to between 0.30 and 0.34 at 80. It usually falls step by step, but a single step can tick up (15 to 30 did in one of our runs), because t-SNE is chaotic. Trustworthiness is 0.94 at perplexity 2 and 0.97 to 0.98 from 5 on. Neighbour purity peaks at 5 to 15 (0.92 to 0.94) and falls to 0.87 or 0.88 at 80.","why":"KL is graded against a different P at every perplexity. Purity shows the real trade-off: a large perplexity starts to merge neighbouring digits."},{"do":"Compare the digit map at perplexity 2 with perplexity 15.","see":"At 2 each digit breaks into many tiny clumps. At 15 most digits are one clump, with a few still split in two.","why":"This is the answer to the quiz: when t-SNE splits one real cluster into fragments, <b>raise</b> the perplexity so the fragments see each other and rejoin."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Perplexity is the number of <b>friends who count</b>. σ is how far you must look to find them: short in a hostel, long in a village."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Perplexity = e<sup>entropy of the weights</sup>. If the weight is shared equally among k points, it is exactly k. That is why it reads as a neighbour count."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Pick a perplexity below the size of the smallest group you care about and well below n. Typical range 5 to 50. Run three values and trust only what survives all three."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"After the search, \"Perplexity achieved\" must equal the target. If it does not, the value was capped or the search failed, and the map answers a different question than you asked."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Never rank settings by KL. Compare trustworthiness and purity, and look at the maps."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>TSNE(perplexity=30)</code> sets the target. After <code>fit_transform</code>, <code>tsne.kl_divergence_</code> holds the final KL."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"Perplexity is the number of neighbours, like k in k-NN.\"","right":"It is a soft, effective count. Every point gets some weight; far ones get almost none. 30 means \"about 30 points matter\"."},{"wrong":"\"σ is one number for the whole dataset.\"","right":"σ is found separately for every point. In the simulation it ranges from 0.24 to 1.43 at the same perplexity because density differs."},{"wrong":"\"The run with the lowest KL is the best map.\"","right":"KL fell from about 0.45 at perplexity 2 to about 0.3 at 80, while purity dropped from 0.93 (at 5) to 0.88. Different perplexities give different targets, so their KL values cannot be compared."},{"wrong":"\"Perplexity 30 is the right setting.\"","right":"30 is only the default. A group smaller than the perplexity gets blended into its surroundings, and a perplexity of 2 shatters real groups."}],"code":[{"title":"Perplexity of one point, from its distances","py":"import numpy as np\n\nd = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0])            # distances from point i to its four neighbours\n\ndef perplexity(sigma):\n    w = np.exp(-d**2 / (2 * sigma**2))         # Gaussian weight of each neighbour\n    p = w / w.sum()                            # conditional probabilities p(j|i): they add to 1\n    H = -(p * np.log(p)).sum()                 # entropy of that distribution, in nats\n    return np.exp(H), p                        # perplexity = e^H = \"effective number of neighbours\"\n\nfor s in (1, 2, 3, 4):\n    print(\"sigma\", s, \"-> perplexity\", round(perplexity(s)[0], 2))\nprint(\"p at sigma 3:\", np.round(perplexity(3)[1], 3))","out":"sigma 1 -> perplexity 1.61\nsigma 2 -> perplexity 2.48\nsigma 3 -> perplexity 3.01\nsigma 4 -> perplexity 3.42\np at sigma 3: [0.433 0.366 0.188 0.013]","walk":[["np.exp(-d**2 / (2 * sigma**2))","The bell-curve weight. <code>d**2</code> squares every distance at once (numpy works on whole arrays), and the division is also elementwise. Result: one weight per neighbour."],["p = w / w.sum()","Divide by the total so the numbers behave like probabilities. <code>w.sum()</code> adds the four weights."],["H = -(p * np.log(p)).sum()","Entropy in nats (natural log). It is large when p is spread out and small when one neighbour takes everything. The minus sign makes it positive because log p is negative."],["return np.exp(H), p","Perplexity is e<sup>H</sup>. A function may return two values; the caller picks one with <code>[0]</code> or <code>[1]</code>."],["for s in (1, 2, 3, 4)","Try four widths. Perplexity rises with σ, from 1 towards the number of neighbours (4). t-SNE does the opposite: given a target perplexity it searches for σ."]],"tip":"Check the code against the table above: σ = 3 must give 3.01. If it does not, you have a typo in the formula."},{"title":"KL changes with perplexity, trustworthiness hardly does","py":"from sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE, trustworthiness\n\nX, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)\nX = X[:300]                                    # 300 images, 64 pixel features each\n\nres = {}\nfor perp in (5, 30):\n    tsne = TSNE(perplexity=perp, init=\"pca\", random_state=0)\n    E = tsne.fit_transform(X)                  # E has shape (300, 2)\n    res[perp] = (tsne.kl_divergence_, trustworthiness(X, E, n_neighbors=10))\n\nprint(\"KL fell from perplexity 5 to 30:\", res[30][0] < res[5][0])\nprint(\"trust moved by less than 0.02:\", abs(res[30][1] - res[5][1]) < 0.02)","out":"KL fell from perplexity 5 to 30: True\ntrust moved by less than 0.02: True","walk":[["load_digits(return_X_y=True)","Loads 1,797 handwritten digits as <code>X</code> (pixels, 64 columns) and <code>y</code> (the digit). <code>return_X_y=True</code> gives the two arrays directly instead of a bundle."],["X[:300]","Keep the first 300 rows to keep t-SNE quick. The colon-slice takes rows 0 to 299."],["TSNE(perplexity=perp, init=\"pca\", random_state=0)","Build the model. <code>perplexity</code> is the target from this lesson. <code>init=\"pca\"</code> and <code>random_state</code> make the run repeatable."],["tsne.kl_divergence_","The final KL, stored after fitting (trailing underscore: a learned value). It is graded against this run's own P."],["res[perp] = (kl, trust)","A dictionary keyed by perplexity, each value a pair. Later <code>res[30][0]</code> is the KL at perplexity 30."],["abs(a - b) < 0.02","True when the two trust scores are within 0.02. We print True/False because the exact values differ a little between scikit-learn versions."]],"tip":"The first print says KL changed a lot, the second says the map's local quality hardly did. That gap is the reason not to use KL to pick perplexity."}],"check":[{"q":"Which description of perplexity is correct?","options":["The largest distance at which two points still count as neighbours","The number of iterations t-SNE runs","The learning rate of the optimiser","The effective number of neighbours of a point, reached by choosing σ separately for every point"],"answer":3,"why":"Perplexity = e<sup>H</sup> of a point's neighbour weights. The search tunes each point's σ until this equals the number you set, which is why dense and sparse regions get different σ."},{"q":"A point has four neighbours with weights 0.25, 0.25, 0.25 and 0.25. What is its perplexity?","options":["2","1","4","0.25"],"answer":2,"why":"H = −4 × 0.25 × ln 0.25 = ln 4, so e<sup>H</sup> = 4. Equal weights on k neighbours always give perplexity k."},{"q":"You run t-SNE on the digits at perplexity 5 (KL 0.42) and at perplexity 50 (KL 0.35). A friend says \"50 is better, its KL is lower.\" What is the problem with that argument?","options":["KL can never be lower at a higher perplexity","Nothing, lower KL is always better","Each perplexity defines a different target P, so the two KL values cannot be compared","KL is only valid below perplexity 30"],"answer":2,"why":"KL measures the match to P, and P changes with perplexity. Compare maps with something independent of P, such as trustworthiness or neighbour purity. Purity here was higher at perplexity 5 than at 50."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend what perplexity does in t-SNE and why you cannot choose it by looking at KL.","model":"Perplexity tells t-SNE how many neighbours should matter to each point: it widens or narrows every point's bell curve until its weights are spread over about that many points, so dense and sparse regions get the same effective neighbourhood size. KL measures the match to a target that itself depends on the perplexity, so values from different perplexities are not comparable and you must judge maps with trustworthiness, purity and your eyes."},"cheat":"t-SNE gives each point a Gaussian over its neighbours and tunes the width σ per point until the perplexity e<sup>H</sup> equals the setting, i.e. about that many neighbours count. The map copies these weights (symmetrised, with a Student-t in 2-D) by minimising KL. KL depends on the perplexity, so compare settings with trustworthiness and purity. Too low fragments clusters, too high merges them."},"embeddings/tsne-seeds":{"idea":"Run t-SNE twice with different random seeds and each point keeps almost the same neighbours, but where whole clusters sit on the page, how far apart they are and which way up the picture is all change. Local structure repeats, global layout does not.","why":"People read a t-SNE picture like a map: \"cluster A is next to B, so they are related\". If another seed puts A next to C, that story was an accident of the seed. Knowing what repeats lets you report only findings that survive a re-run.","analogy":"Seating 300 guests at a wedding with one rule: families sit together. Two planners can both keep every family together, so the local structure is the same, yet they put the families at different tables in a different order. Turning the hall's floor plan by 90 degrees changes nothing at all. Only the first kind of structure is a fact about the guests.","vocab":[["Seed (random_state)","A number that fixes the random choices. Same data, same settings and same seed give the same picture. A different seed gives a different random start."],["Initialisation","The starting positions of the 2-D points. <b>Random</b>: all points in a tiny random blob. <b>PCA</b>: the first two PCA coordinates, scaled very small. The start is the only thing the seed changes."],["Local minimum","A layout where no small move lowers the cost, though a better layout exists elsewhere. Different starts end in different ones, all about equally good."],["Between-cluster distance","The distance between the centres of two digit clusters in the map. With 10 digits there are 45 such pairs."],["Agreement (r)","The correlation between the 45 between-cluster distances of two runs. 1 means identical relative spacing, near 0 means unrelated."],["Procrustes alignment","Rotating, flipping and shifting one map to lie as close as possible to another. It removes accidental turns and nothing else."],["Trustworthiness","0 to 1: the share of each point's map neighbours that were real neighbours in the 64 pixels. High means local structure is right."]],"steps":[{"h":"A random start","p":"t-SNE begins with all 300 points in a tiny random blob, about 0.0001 wide, and lets forces pull neighbours together and push everything else apart. A different seed means a different blob and so a different path downhill."},{"h":"Clusters form, then drift","p":"In the first 250 steps (early exaggeration) the neighbour attraction is boosted 12 times. Groups of similar digits snap together as clumps. After that the clumps are pushed apart by the repulsion between all pairs and slide until nothing moves."},{"h":"Many layouts are equally good","p":"Rotating or flipping a map leaves every distance, and so the cost, unchanged. Swapping where two far clusters sit costs almost nothing either: far pairs have P near 0, so they add almost nothing to KL. The objective does not pin down the arrangement of clusters."},{"h":"What the data does pin down","p":"Each point's near neighbours have large P, so every run must keep them together. That is why trustworthiness hardly changes between seeds: 0.980, 0.975 and 0.974 in one run of the page, all within 0.01."},{"h":"What the seed decides","p":"Where each cluster lands and how far from the others. To measure it, take the centre of each digit's points in the map, compute the 45 centre-to-centre distances, and correlate them between two runs. That is agreement r. In one run of the page r was 0.55, 0.59 and 0.23 for the three pairs, mean 0.45. Another batch gave a mean of 0.74."},{"h":"Alignment helps only a little","p":"Rotation and flips do not change distances, so aligning cannot change r. It only makes the pictures easier to compare by eye. The clusters that stay in different places after aligning are a real difference between the runs, not a trick of rotation."},{"h":"PCA start: the randomness is removed, not the sensitivity","p":"With init PCA, every run starts at the same point, so the three runs here are identical (r = 1.00). That is steady, not trustworthy: you see one of many equally good layouts. Change the perplexity or add some points and the arrangement can still change."},{"h":"A working rule","p":"Run at least three seeds. Report only what repeats: which digits sit next to which, which clumps stay whole. Do not report the distance between clusters, their order, or the orientation. If you need one fixed layout, use PCA start and say it is one of many."}],"example":{"title":"agreement r for four digits, by hand","setup":"Take digits 0, 1, 2 and 3 from two runs of the simulation. The centres of their clumps are 6 pairs apart. Here are those 6 distances in run 1 and in run 2 (1 decimal). These come from one machine: yours will differ, because t-SNE is chaotic and browsers round slightly differently. The arithmetic is the same. Does run 2 keep the same spacing?","table":{"head":["Pair","Run 1","Run 2","Run 1 − 23.37","Run 2 − 23.32","Product"],"rows":[["0 and 1","31.5","25.8","8.1","2.5","20.2"],["0 and 2","41.0","29.9","17.6","6.6","116.1"],["0 and 3","31.5","18.9","8.1","−4.4","−35.9"],["1 and 2","11.4","27.3","−12.0","4.0","−47.7"],["1 and 3","12.4","26.1","−11.0","2.8","−30.5"],["2 and 3","12.4","11.9","−11.0","−11.4","125.2"]]},"walk":["Means: run 1 gives (31.5 + 41.0 + 31.5 + 11.4 + 12.4 + 12.4)/6 = 140.2/6 = 23.37. Run 2 gives 139.9/6 = 23.32.","Subtract each mean from its column (columns 4 and 5). Multiply the two deviations in each row (last column).","Sum of products: 20.2 + 116.1 − 35.9 − 47.7 − 30.5 + 125.2 = <b>147.4</b>.","Sum of squared deviations for run 1: 8.1² + 17.6² + 8.1² + 12.0² + 11.0² + 11.0² = <b>827.0</b>. For run 2: 2.5² + 6.6² + 4.4² + 4.0² + 2.8² + 11.4² = <b>223.0</b>.","r = 147.4 / √(827.0 × 223.0) = 147.4 / 429.4 = <b>0.34</b>.","Read it: in run 1 digits 1, 2 and 3 are close together (11 to 12 apart) while 0 is far from all. In run 2 digit 1 has moved away from 2 and 3 (27.3 and 26.1) while 2 and 3 stay together (11.9). The spacing is quite different, although each digit is the same tight clump in both."],"result":"With 10 digits and 45 distances the page reports the mean of three such r values. One run gave 0.55, 0.59 and 0.23, mean 0.45. Compare that with trustworthiness at 0.97 to 0.98. Neighbourhoods repeat, spacing between clusters does not. A bigger r would need the spacing to match: r = 1 even if one map is 10 times bigger, because r ignores scale."},"read":[["Three map cards: Run 1 · seed 1, Run 2 · seed 2, Run 3 · seed 3","The same 300 digits (30 per class) drawn as their digit, coloured by class, from three different random starts. With PCA start the titles read \"PCA start\"."],["Readout: Trustworthiness of runs 1, 2, 3","Local quality of each map. Near-equal values mean the runs agree on neighbourhoods."],["Readout: Agreement of between-cluster distances (mean r)","The mean of the three pairwise correlations. It turns green above 0.9 and amber below."],["Readout: Run 1 vs 2 / 1 vs 3 / 2 vs 3","The three correlations one by one."],["Segmented control: Initial positions","<i>Random</i>: tiny random blob (the seed matters). <i>PCA</i>: start from PCA coordinates (the seed has no effect)."],["Checkbox: Align maps to run 1 (rotate / flip)","Redraws maps 2 and 3 turned or flipped to lie as near as possible to map 1. It changes only the drawing; the readouts stay the same."],["Slider: Perplexity (5 to 60)","The t-SNE neighbourhood size, same as in the perplexity lesson."],["Button: Run again","Adds 3 to each seed (so 4, 5, 6 after the first press) and reruns all three maps."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the poll, then look at the three maps. In each map find the clump of digit 3 and the clump of digit 9, and then name the clump nearest to digit 2.","see":"The 3 and 9 clumps are neighbours in nearly every run (over 12 seeds each was the other's nearest clump 11 or 12 times). Digit 2's nearest clump changes: over the same 12 seeds it was 1, 3, 5, 9, 7 or 8, and none of them more than 3 times.","why":"Two very similar groups (3 and 9) have large P between them and must stay together. A group with several moderately similar neighbours is free to settle next to any of them."},{"do":"Read <i>Trustworthiness of runs 1, 2, 3</i> and the agreement. Then press <i>Run again</i> four or five times.","see":"Trustworthiness stays between 0.97 and 0.98 every time. Mean r wanders from batch to batch, roughly between 0.45 and 0.75 (0.45, 0.46, 0.74, 0.65, 0.54 in one of our sessions), and single pairs run from about 0.2 to 0.8.","why":"The neighbourhoods are fixed by the data, the arrangement is decided by the random start, so one batch of three runs is a noisy sample of how arbitrary the layout is."},{"do":"Switch <i>Initial positions</i> to <i>PCA</i>.","see":"A note on the page says so: the three maps are identical, trustworthiness is the same three times (about 0.98), mean r reads 1.00 and the pairwise line ends with \"(identical runs)\".","why":"PCA gives every run the same start and nothing else is random, so the three runs follow the same path. This hides the arbitrariness, it does not remove it."},{"do":"Switch back to <i>Random</i> and tick <i>Align maps to run 1</i>.","see":"Maps 2 and 3 are turned or flipped to match map 1 as closely as possible. Every clump keeps its shape. The pictures look more alike, yet clusters still sit in different places, and the agreement readout does not change.","why":"Rotations and flips leave every distance unchanged, so r cannot change. What remains after alignment is the real difference in arrangement."},{"do":"Back on <i>Random</i>, press <i>Run again</i> four or five times at perplexity 30 and write down each batch's mean r. Repeat at perplexity 5.","see":"Mean r runs from about 0.45 to 0.75 over five batches at perplexity 30 and from about 0.4 to 0.6 at perplexity 5. The ranges overlap. Trustworthiness is a little lower at 5 (about 0.97 against 0.98).","why":"The batch-to-batch noise in r (0.4 to 0.9) is bigger than any effect of perplexity. At every setting we tried, r stayed far below 1."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Wedding seating</b>: families together (reliable), tables in a different order each time (arbitrary)."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Trust <b>who is next to whom</b> inside and between near clumps. Do not trust the distance between far clusters, their order or the orientation."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Rerun with 3 seeds. A statement that appears in all three is a finding. A statement that appears in one is decoration."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Before writing \"A is closer to B than to C\", change the seed. If the claim flips, it came from the seed, not from the data."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Agreement r = correlation of the 45 centre-to-centre distances of two maps. It ignores scale and is unchanged by rotation, flips and shifts."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>TSNE(random_state=s)</code> sets the seed. <code>init=\"pca\"</code> makes the start (and so the result) the same for every seed."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"Different pictures from different seeds mean the algorithm is broken, or one of them is wrong.\"","right":"Both are valid. They have the same neighbourhoods (trustworthiness within about 0.01) and differ only in what the cost function barely constrains: the arrangement of clusters."},{"wrong":"\"With PCA initialisation the layout is now reliable.\"","right":"It is repeatable, not reliable. All three runs are identical because their start is identical. One arrangement out of many equally good ones is still arbitrary."},{"wrong":"\"Procrustes alignment makes two maps agree.\"","right":"It removes rotation, flips and shifts only, which never changed the distances in the first place. It cannot move one cluster relative to another."},{"wrong":"\"Setting random_state=42 makes my picture the true layout.\"","right":"A fixed seed makes the picture repeatable. It does not make the arrangement mean anything."}],"code":[{"title":"Same neighbours, different spacing","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom itertools import combinations\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE, trustworthiness\n\nX, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)\npick = np.random.RandomState(0).choice(len(y), 300, replace=False)   # 300 random images\nX, y = X[pick], y[pick]\ncentres = lambda E: np.array([E[y == k].mean(axis=0) for k in range(10)])        # one centre per digit\ngaps = lambda E: [np.linalg.norm(a - b) for a, b in combinations(centres(E), 2)]  # the 45 centre gaps\nruns = [TSNE(init=\"random\", method=\"exact\", random_state=s).fit_transform(X) for s in range(3)]\nprint(\"every run trusted (> 0.95):\", all(trustworthiness(X, E, n_neighbors=10) > 0.95 for E in runs))\nr = [np.corrcoef(gaps(runs[i]), gaps(runs[j]))[0, 1] for i, j in combinations(range(3), 2)]\nprint(\"mean agreement of centre gaps below 0.8:\", np.mean(r) < 0.8)","out":"every run trusted (> 0.95): True\nmean agreement of centre gaps below 0.8: True","walk":[["np.random.RandomState(0).choice(len(y), 300, replace=False)","Pick 300 different row numbers at random. <code>RandomState(0)</code> fixes the draw. <code>replace=False</code> means no image is picked twice."],["X, y = X[pick], y[pick]","Index with the list of row numbers to keep the chosen images and their digit labels in step."],["E[y == k].mean(axis=0)","<code>y == k</code> is a True/False mask of the images of digit k. <code>E[mask]</code> keeps their map rows, and <code>.mean(axis=0)</code> averages down the rows to give the cluster centre (x, y). <code>centres</code> stacks the ten centres into a 10 by 2 array."],["combinations(centres(E), 2)","Every unordered pair of rows, that is every pair of digit centres: (0,1), (0,2) ... (8,9). That is 45 pairs. <code>gaps</code> turns each pair into one distance with <code>np.linalg.norm(a - b)</code>."],["TSNE(init=\"random\", method=\"exact\", random_state=s)","Random start, so the seed changes the result. <code>method=\"exact\"</code> computes all pairs (fine for 300 points) and matches the simulation."],["np.corrcoef(a, b)[0, 1]","Returns a 2 by 2 matrix of correlations; entry [0, 1] is the correlation between the two lists. That is agreement r."]],"tip":"Same trust, low r: that pair of lines is the whole lesson in code."},{"title":"PCA start removes the randomness","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\n\nX = load_digits().data\nX = X[np.random.RandomState(0).choice(len(X), 300, replace=False)]\n\ndef same_map(init):                            # fit twice with different seeds and compare the coordinates\n    a = TSNE(init=init, random_state=0).fit_transform(X)\n    b = TSNE(init=init, random_state=1).fit_transform(X)\n    return bool(np.allclose(a, b, atol=1e-2))\n\nprint(\"PCA start gives the same map:\", same_map(\"pca\"))\nprint(\"random start gives the same map:\", same_map(\"random\"))","out":"PCA start gives the same map: True\nrandom start gives the same map: False","walk":[["load_digits().data","The 64-pixel images as a 1,797 by 64 array, without the labels (we do not need them here)."],["def same_map(init):","A function so the same test runs twice with different <code>init</code> choices."],["TSNE(init=init, random_state=0)","Seed 0 for the first fit and seed 1 for the second. Only the seed differs."],["np.allclose(a, b, atol=1e-2)","True when every coordinate of the two maps agrees to within 0.01. Maps are around 50 wide, so this is a very strict test."],["bool(...)","Turns numpy's True into Python's, so the output prints as plain <code>True</code>."]],"tip":"If you need the same picture tomorrow, set both <code>init=\"pca\"</code> and <code>random_state</code>."},{"title":"What alignment can and cannot fix","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.linalg import orthogonal_procrustes\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nA = rng.randn(200, 2)                          # a pretend 2-D map\nturned = A @ np.array([[0, -1], [1, 0]])       # the same map turned by 90 degrees\nother = rng.randn(200, 2)                      # a genuinely different layout\n\ndef gap_after_aligning(P, Q):                  # leftover distance after the best rotation or flip\n    R, _ = orthogonal_procrustes(P, Q)         # R is a 2x2 rotation/reflection matrix\n    return round(float(np.linalg.norm(P @ R - Q)), 1)\n\nprint(\"turned copy:\", gap_after_aligning(turned, A))\nprint(\"different layout:\", gap_after_aligning(other, A))","out":"turned copy: 0.0\ndifferent layout: 26.4","walk":[["rng.randn(200, 2)","200 random points with 2 coordinates each: a stand-in for a map."],["A @ np.array([[0, -1], [1, 0]])","<code>@</code> is matrix multiplication. This particular matrix rotates every point by 90 degrees."],["orthogonal_procrustes(P, Q)","Finds the rotation or flip matrix <code>R</code> that brings P as close as possible to Q. It returns a pair (R, scale); we keep only R and write <code>_</code> for the part we ignore."],["P @ R - Q","Apply the best rotation to P, subtract Q, and measure what is left with <code>np.linalg.norm</code>."],["round(float(...), 1)","Convert to a plain number and keep one decimal so the output is short."]],"tip":"A turned copy aligns to 0.0. Two different layouts keep a large gap. This is exactly what you see when you tick <i>Align maps to run 1</i>."}],"check":[{"q":"Two t-SNE runs on the same data use different seeds. Which of these is most likely to differ?","options":["The distance between two far-apart clusters","Which points are among each point's 10 nearest neighbours","The number of points","The trustworthiness score to within 0.01"],"answer":0,"why":"Near neighbours are fixed by large P, so trustworthiness and neighbour lists repeat. The spacing of far clusters is barely constrained by the cost and depends on the random start."},{"q":"Run A gives centre distances (2, 4, 6) for three pairs of digits. Run B, drawn on a map ten times larger, gives (20, 40, 60). What is the agreement r?","options":["1.0","0.1","0.5","10"],"answer":0,"why":"B is exactly 10 times A, a perfectly straight-line relation, so the correlation is 1. Correlation ignores scale, which is what you want when two maps use different units."},{"q":"You tick <i>Align maps to run 1 (rotate / flip)</i>. What happens to the readout <i>Agreement of between-cluster distances</i>?","options":["It drops towards 0","It rises to 1.00","It becomes undefined","It does not change, since rotations and flips do not change distances"],"answer":3,"why":"The centre-to-centre distances are the same before and after a rotation or flip, so r is identical. Alignment is only for comparing the drawings by eye."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend what stays the same and what changes when you run t-SNE with different seeds, and why.","model":"Each point keeps nearly the same neighbours, so trustworthiness hardly changes, because the data fixes which points must be close. Where whole clusters sit, how far apart they are and which way the picture is turned depend on the random start, because the cost barely cares about the position of far-apart clusters, so I should only trust neighbours and not distances between clusters."},"cheat":"Different seeds give different random starts and so different t-SNE layouts of equal quality. Neighbourhoods repeat (trustworthiness within about 0.01), but the spacing between clusters does not (mean agreement r about 0.4 to 0.75). Rotation or flip alignment cannot fix that. PCA start makes runs identical without making the layout meaningful. Report only what survives three seeds."},"embeddings/misreading":{"idea":"A 2-D map shows who is near whom. It does not show how spread out a cluster is or how far apart two clusters are, and with a small neighbourhood it will draw clumps in pure noise. Measure sizes and distances in the original features.","why":"A report that says \"segment 2 is the most varied\" or \"these two groups are far apart\" from a t-SNE or UMAP picture can be wrong by a factor of 4 or more. Decisions about which customers to target or merge then rest on how the picture was drawn, not on the data.","analogy":"The Delhi Metro map. Stations are drawn at equal spacing and the lines at tidy angles, though the real gaps differ a lot. The map is correct about the order of stations and where you change trains, and useless for how many kilometres you travel. A t-SNE map is the same kind of drawing: neighbour relations are right, sizes and gaps were redrawn to fit the page.","vocab":[["Spread","How loose a group is in the original features: the mean distance from its points to their centre. This is the true size of a cluster."],["Apparent size","How wide the same group looks in the 2-D map."],["Local scale normalisation","Both methods rescale each point's neighbourhood by its own distances (σ in t-SNE, ρ and σ in UMAP). A loose cluster and a tight one then look the same to the method."],["Distance concentration","With many features, nearly all pairs of random points are almost equally far apart. With 50 noise features the typical distance is 10.0 give or take 1.0."],["Structure from nothing","Clumps or strands in a map of data that has no clusters. Made by the method, not found in the data."],["Null test","Run the same method with the same settings on data with the structure destroyed (shuffle each column on its own) to see what pure artefacts look like."],["Perplexity","The effective neighbour count of t-SNE. Small values make each point commit to a very few neighbours."]],"steps":[{"h":"The set-up","p":"Three customer segments, 10 features. Segment 1: 240 customers, spread 0.5 per feature. Segment 2: 40 customers, spread 2.0, so four times looser. Segment 3: 40 customers, spread 0.5. Truly, Segment 2 is the loose one: its customers are on average 6.3 from their centre, against 1.5 for the others."},{"h":"The method only sees neighbour weights","p":"t-SNE and UMAP first turn distances into weights between neighbours, and the map is built from those weights alone. If two clusters give the same weights, the map cannot tell them apart. So the question is: does stretching a cluster change its weights?"},{"h":"It does not","p":"A point's weight on a neighbour is exp(−d²/2σ²), and σ is searched so that the neighbour count stays at the perplexity. Stretch the cluster 4 times and d and σ both grow 4 times, so d/σ and every weight stay the same. UMAP's exp(−(d − ρ)/σ) behaves the same way. Loose and tight clusters produce identical weights."},{"h":"So who sets the size on the page?","p":"The head-count. Each point wants a similar amount of room around it, so 240 points need more space than 40. In the page Segment 1 is drawn about 4 times wider than Segment 2 or 3, in both methods. Give all three segments 40 customers and all three are drawn the same size, even though one is 4 times looser."},{"h":"Gaps between clusters are also redrawn","p":"Far-apart clusters have almost no neighbour weight between them, so the data says very little about the gap. It is set by repulsion and the random start. In the page, t-SNE draws three well-separated segments almost equally far apart (0.91 to 1.16 of the average gap) although the true gaps range from 0.65 to 1.38."},{"h":"Noise has neighbours too","p":"Take 300 points with 50 independent noise features. Every pair is about 10.0 ± 1.0 apart, and the nearest neighbour is at 7.5, only 25% closer than a typical point. Perplexity 2 makes every point commit to its two nearest, however weakly related, and tiny clumps and chains appear. Perplexity 30 or 80 averages over many points and gives a featureless disc."},{"h":"What to do instead","p":"Measure sizes and distances in the original features, using the same recipe as the page's \"true\" columns. Run a null test: shuffle each column and rerun the same settings, and be suspicious of any clump that also appears there. Change the seed and the perplexity, and keep only what survives."}],"example":{"title":"a loose cluster and a tight one give the same weights","setup":"Tight cluster: a point has four neighbours at distances 1, 2, 4, 8, and perplexity 3 needs σ = 3 (see the perplexity lesson). Loose cluster: the same arrangement stretched 4 times, distances 4, 8, 16, 32. What σ does the search find, and what weights come out?","table":{"head":["Cluster","Distances d","σ for perplexity 3","Weights exp(−d²/2σ²)","p(j|i)"],"rows":[["Tight","1, 2, 4, 8","3","0.946, 0.801, 0.411, 0.029","0.433, 0.366, 0.188, 0.013"],["Loose (4 times)","4, 8, 16, 32","12","0.946, 0.801, 0.411, 0.029","0.433, 0.366, 0.188, 0.013"]]},"walk":["Tight cluster, σ = 3: weights exp(−d²/18) = exp(−0.056), exp(−0.222), exp(−0.889), exp(−3.556) = 0.946, 0.801, 0.411, 0.029. They add to 2.186, so p = 0.433, 0.366, 0.188, 0.013 and perplexity 3.01.","Loose cluster, try σ = 12: weights exp(−d²/288) = exp(−16/288), exp(−64/288), exp(−256/288), exp(−1024/288) = exp(−0.056), exp(−0.222), exp(−0.889), exp(−3.556). The exponents are the same numbers as before.","Why: d²/(2σ²) becomes (4d)²/(2·(4σ)²) = 16d²/(16·2σ²), and the 16 cancels. Only the ratio d/σ matters. The search for σ finds 4 times the old value, so the weights, the entropy and the perplexity are unchanged.","In the widget's own t-SNE code, multiplying a whole 40-point dataset by 4 gives exactly the same table of pair weights P (largest difference 0).","UMAP's memberships exp(−(d − ρ)/σ) scale in the same way: ρ and σ grow 4 times with d, so the ratio (d − ρ)/σ is unchanged.","So the map built from these weights has no way to know the second cluster was four times looser. What is left to set its size on the page is how many points it has."],"result":"Identical weights, identical map. On the page's default data the spread of each segment relative to the average of the three is: true 0.49, 2.03, 0.48; t-SNE map 2.04, 0.48, 0.48; UMAP map about 1.95, 0.52, 0.54. The map shows nearly the true picture with Segments 1 and 2 swapped: the 240-customer tight segment looks widest and the genuinely loose segment looks tight."},"read":[["Segmented control: Show","<i>Cluster sizes</i>: the customer experiment. <i>Structure from nothing</i>: the noise experiment. The poll belongs to the first one only."],["Cards: t-SNE (perplexity 30) and UMAP (n_neighbors 15, min_dist 0.1)","The same 320 customers, one marker shape and colour per segment. The legend gives the head-count of each segment."],["Table: Apparent spread against true spread","Spread = mean distance of a customer to its segment centre. The \"true\" column measures it in the 10 features, the other two in the maps. Each column is divided by its own average over the three segments, so 1.00× is average and each column adds to 3.00×."],["Table: Distance between segment centres","The same comparison for the three gaps between segment centres: true distance in 10-D, then in the t-SNE map and the UMAP map, each relative to its own average."],["Six cards in Structure from nothing","Maps of the same 300 noise points: t-SNE with perplexity 2, 5, 30, 80 and UMAP with n_neighbors 5 and 50. Every dot is noise."],["Button: New seed","Draws new customers or new noise and restarts the runs."],["Progress bar and Animation control","The maps are drawn while they settle. Wait for the bar to finish before reading the tables."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Answer the poll before running anything: which of the three segments will look widest?","see":"Segment 1, the 240-customer tight one, even though Segment 2 is the loose one.","why":"The maps give a crowd room in proportion to its head-count and cannot see the spread, as the example shows."},{"do":"In <i>Cluster sizes</i> compare the three columns of the first table. Then press <i>New seed</i> five times.","see":"The true column reads about 0.5, 2.0, 0.5 every time. The t-SNE column reads about 2.05, 0.48, 0.47 and the UMAP column about 2.0, 0.5, 0.5, with a little more wobble (up to 0.05) in each.","why":"The loose segment is the one the map shrinks. It is drawn as tight as the genuinely tight Segment 3 because the weights are the same."},{"do":"Read the second table (distance between segment centres) for the default data, then press <i>New seed</i> a few times.","see":"True gaps vary from seed to seed (from 0.65 to 1.38 of the average over six seeds). t-SNE stays close to 1.00 each time (0.91 to 1.16), whatever the truth. UMAP moves more (about 0.8 to 1.3) but does not follow the true gaps either.","why":"The data says little about the gap between far clusters, so the method fills in a tidy layout. Do not read \"far\" or \"near\" off a map between clusters."},{"do":"Switch <i>Show</i> to <i>Structure from nothing</i>. Compare the t-SNE maps at perplexity 2, 5, 30 and 80, then the two UMAP maps.","see":"Perplexity 2 shows many small clumps and chains. Perplexity 5 hints at some. Perplexity 30 and 80 are featureless discs. UMAP with n_neighbors 5 shows faint short strands, n_neighbors 50 a disc.","why":"With 50 noise features the nearest neighbour is only 25% closer than a typical point. A tiny neighbourhood has to commit to these accidental neighbours and draws them as groups. A large neighbourhood averages them out."},{"do":"Press <i>New seed</i> three or four times in <i>Structure from nothing</i>.","see":"The clumps in the perplexity-2 map appear in new places each time, while the perplexity-80 map stays a disc.","why":"Structure that moves when only the random draw changes was never in the data. A finding must survive a new seed."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>The map communicates; the original space measures.</b> Compute spreads and distances from the features, then draw the map to show them."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"A <b>metro map</b>: right about order and connections, wrong about kilometres."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"<b>Null test.</b> Shuffle each column independently (this keeps every feature's distribution but kills the structure), rerun with the same settings and compare. Clumps that appear in both are artefacts."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Divide the typical nearest-neighbour distance by the typical distance. With many features, pure noise gives about 0.76 (50 features), the digits about 0.37 and well-separated tight clusters 0.1 or less. A value near 0.76 says \"no clusters to find\". It is meaningful only with many features."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Equal-looking clusters in a map do not mean equal clusters in the data. Head-count shows up as size, loose and tight look the same."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"True spread of cluster k: <code>np.linalg.norm(X[y==k] - X[y==k].mean(0), axis=1).mean()</code>, computed on X, never on the map."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The widest cluster on the map is the most spread out group.\"","right":"On this page the 240-customer tight segment is drawn widest and the genuinely loose segment narrowest. Apparent size follows head-count."},{"wrong":"\"Two clusters far apart on the map are more different than two close ones.\"","right":"Gaps between far clusters are redrawn. Over six seeds t-SNE drew the three gaps almost equal while the true gaps ranged from 0.65 to 1.38 times the average."},{"wrong":"\"UMAP keeps global structure, so I can read sizes off a UMAP map.\"","right":"UMAP also rescales every neighbourhood by its own distances. Its spread column reads about 1.95, 0.52, 0.54 against the true 0.49, 2.03, 0.48: no better than t-SNE."},{"wrong":"\"The algorithm found these clumps, so they must be real.\"","right":"At perplexity 2 it also finds them in 300 points of pure noise, in a different place each time. Run a null test and change the seed."}],"code":[{"title":"Stretching a cluster does not change the weights","py":"import numpy as np\n\ndef p_row(d, sigma):                           # conditional probabilities of one point, as in t-SNE\n    w = np.exp(-d**2 / (2 * sigma**2))\n    return w / w.sum()\n\ntight = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0])         # distances to four neighbours in a tight cluster\nloose = 4 * tight                              # the same cluster stretched 4 times: a loose cluster\nprint(np.round(p_row(tight, 3), 3))\nprint(np.round(p_row(loose, 12), 3))           # the perplexity search finds a sigma 4 times larger\nprint(\"identical:\", np.allclose(p_row(tight, 3), p_row(loose, 12)))","out":"[0.433 0.366 0.188 0.013]\n[0.433 0.366 0.188 0.013]\nidentical: True","walk":[["def p_row(d, sigma)","One point's neighbour probabilities from its distances <code>d</code> and bell width <code>sigma</code>. The same formula as in the perplexity lesson."],["loose = 4 * tight","Multiplying a numpy array by 4 multiplies every entry, so every distance is 4 times larger."],["p_row(loose, 12)","σ is 4 times larger too, which is what the perplexity search would pick. We pass it directly to keep the code short."],["np.allclose(a, b)","True when two arrays agree up to tiny rounding errors. Use it instead of <code>==</code> for decimals."]],"tip":"If you ever want a method to respond to cluster size, it must use distances without dividing by a local scale. t-SNE and UMAP do not."},{"title":"Equal head-count: the loose cluster is drawn as tight as the others","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\n\ndef blobs(sizes, stds):                        # three segments in 10 features, centres far apart\n    rng = np.random.RandomState(0)\n    y = np.repeat([0, 1, 2], sizes)\n    return rng.uniform(-12, 12, (3, 10))[y] + rng.randn(len(y), 10) * np.array(stds)[y][:, None], y\ndef relative_spread(E, y):                     # mean distance to own centre, divided by the average of the three\n    s = [np.linalg.norm(E[y == k] - E[y == k].mean(0), axis=1).mean() for k in range(3)]\n    return np.round(np.array(s) / np.mean(s), 1)\nfor sizes in ([40, 40, 40], [240, 40, 40]):\n    X, y = blobs(sizes, [0.5, 2.0, 0.5])\n    print(sizes, \"true\", relative_spread(X, y), \"map\", relative_spread(TSNE(random_state=0).fit_transform(X), y))","out":"[40, 40, 40] true [0.5 2.  0.5] map [1. 1. 1.]\n[240, 40, 40] true [0.5 2.  0.5] map [2.1 0.5 0.5]","walk":[["np.repeat([0, 1, 2], sizes)","The label of every customer: <code>sizes</code> says how many copies of 0, 1 and 2. With <code>[240, 40, 40]</code> you get 240 zeros, then 40 ones, then 40 twos."],["rng.uniform(-12, 12, (3, 10))[y]","Three random centres in 10 features. Indexing with <code>[y]</code> gives each customer the centre of its own segment: a 320 by 10 array."],["rng.randn(len(y), 10) * np.array(stds)[y][:, None]","Gaussian noise for every customer, scaled by its segment's spread. <code>[:, None]</code> turns the spreads into a column so they multiply row by row."],["np.linalg.norm(E[y == k] - E[y == k].mean(0), axis=1).mean()","For segment k: subtract its centre from each of its rows, take each row's length (<code>axis=1</code>), then average. That is the spread."],["s / np.mean(s)","Divide by the average of the three so that 1.0 means \"average\". The map and the original space use different units, so only relative values can be compared."]],"tip":"The left output shows the cleanest form of the effect: the loose segment is truly 4 times wider, and the map draws all three the same."},{"title":"A quick check for \"is there anything to find?\"","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import pdist, squareform\n\ndef nn_ratio(X):                               # typical nearest-neighbour distance / typical distance\n    D = squareform(pdist(X))\n    np.fill_diagonal(D, np.inf)\n    return round(float(np.median(D.min(axis=1)) / pdist(X).mean()), 2)\n\nrng = np.random.RandomState(0)\nnoise = rng.randn(300, 50)                                  # 50 independent noise features\ncentres = rng.uniform(-12, 12, (3, 50))\nclusters = centres[rng.randint(0, 3, 300)] + 0.5 * rng.randn(300, 50)    # three tight clusters\nprint(\"noise:\", nn_ratio(noise), \" clusters:\", nn_ratio(clusters))","out":"noise: 0.76  clusters: 0.08","walk":[["pdist(X)","All pairwise distances as one flat list (300 points give 44,850 pairs). <code>squareform</code> turns that list into a 300 by 300 table."],["np.fill_diagonal(D, np.inf)","Every point is at distance 0 from itself. Setting the diagonal to infinity stops a point counting as its own nearest neighbour."],["D.min(axis=1)","For each row, the smallest entry: that point's nearest-neighbour distance. <code>np.median</code> of these is the typical one."],["rng.randint(0, 3, 300)","A random choice of cluster (0, 1 or 2) for each of 300 points. Indexing <code>centres[...]</code> with it gives each point its cluster's centre."],["nn_ratio(noise)","0.76: the nearest neighbour is nearly as far as a typical point, so nothing is \"close\". 0.08 for clusters."]],"tip":"This number is meaningful only when there are many features. In 2-D, any random cloud gives a small ratio."}],"check":[{"q":"A t-SNE map shows one segment about twice as wide as another. What can you conclude about their spread in the original features?","options":["Nothing. The wider one may just have more points, and a loose and a tight cluster can look the same. Measure the spread in the features.","The wider segment is about half as spread out","The two segments must have the same number of points","The wider segment is twice as spread out"],"answer":0,"why":"Apparent size follows head-count and the method divides out each neighbourhood's own scale. On the page the 240-customer tight segment looks about 4 times wider than the loose one."},{"q":"A cluster's distances to four neighbours are 1, 2, 4, 8 and the search found σ = 3. A looser cluster has distances 2, 4, 8, 16. Which σ gives the same weights?","options":["6","12","3","1.5"],"answer":0,"why":"Weights depend only on d/σ. The distances are doubled, so σ doubles: 6. (The page's loose segment is 4 times looser, which would need σ = 12.)"},{"q":"You press <i>New seed</i> in <i>Structure from nothing</i>. What do you expect from the perplexity-2 map?","options":["A clear picture of 3 clusters","A featureless disc","Small clumps and chains again, but in different places","The same clumps in the same places"],"answer":2,"why":"The clumps come from each point committing to its two nearest of 299 almost-equally-far points, which are different for different random draws. Structure that moves with the seed is not in the data."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why a t-SNE or UMAP map cannot tell you how spread out a cluster is, and what you should do instead.","model":"The methods rescale every point's neighbourhood by its own distances, so a loose cluster and a tight one give the same neighbour weights and the map has only the number of points left to set a cluster's size. To judge spread or distance I should compute them from the original features and use the map only to show who is near whom."},"cheat":"Maps are built from neighbour weights that are divided by each point's local scale, so loose and tight clusters look alike and apparent size follows head-count (240 against 40 customers looked 4 times wider). Gaps between far clusters are redrawn too. Small perplexity makes clumps in pure noise. Measure size and distance in the features; run a null test and several seeds."},"embeddings/umap-settings":{"idea":"UMAP first joins every point to its nearest neighbours with a strength between 0 and 1, then arranges the points in 2-D so that joined points stay close. n_neighbors decides who is joined. min_dist decides how tightly the map may pack points.","why":"The two settings answer different questions. n_neighbors decides what UMAP believes about your data: how local the structure is. min_dist decides only how the picture looks. Mix them up and you tune the drawing when you meant to change the structure, or the other way round.","analogy":"Each student in a class lists their closest friends. The number on the list is n_neighbors, and a friend who also lists you makes a stronger tie. Now you draw this network on a poster. min_dist is how close two dots may be drawn: nearly touching, or politely spaced. Changing the spacing on the poster never changes who is friends with whom.","vocab":[["n_neighbors","How many nearest points (counting the point itself) define a point's neighbourhood. 15 means the point plus 14 others."],["Membership","A number from 0 to 1 on a link: how strongly a point is tied to a neighbour. The nearest neighbour always gets 1, farther ones fade."],["ρ (rho)","The distance to a point's nearest neighbour. UMAP subtracts it, so every point has at least one full-strength tie and nobody is isolated."],["σ (sigma)","A per-point scale found by search so that the memberships of that point add up to log₂(n_neighbors)."],["Fuzzy union","Merging the two directions of a link, a (i about j) and b (j about i), into one weight w = a + b − ab."],["Neighbour graph","Points are nodes and the memberships are weighted links. n_neighbors alone decides it. UMAP then lays this graph out in 2-D."],["min_dist","The distance in the map below which two points are treated as already close, so they stop attracting each other. 0 allows tight clumps, large values spread points out."],["a, b","Two numbers fitted from min_dist so that the smooth curve 1/(1 + a·d<sup>2b</sup>) follows the target similarity (1 up to min_dist, then falling away)."]],"steps":[{"h":"Neighbours of one point","p":"For point i, find the n_neighbors nearest points. The first is i itself at distance 0. With n_neighbors = 5 that leaves four real neighbours, at distances d₁ ≤ d₂ ≤ d₃ ≤ d₄. This is the only place n_neighbors is used."},{"h":"Fuzzy strengths","p":"Subtract ρ = d₁ so the nearest neighbour gets strength 1. The others get exp(−(d − ρ)/σ). Search σ for this point until the strengths add up to log₂(n_neighbors). As in t-SNE, this equalises dense and sparse regions: a point in a sparse region just gets a wider σ."},{"h":"Merge the two directions","p":"i may list j as a neighbour with strength a, while j lists i with strength b. UMAP combines them as w = a + b − ab. This is \"at least one of the two ties holds\". It is at least max(a, b), and exactly 1 if either is 1."},{"h":"The graph is finished before the map exists","p":"Everything so far depends only on the original data and n_neighbors. The 400 digits at n_neighbors = 15 give 3,592 links, about 18 per point, however you set min_dist. Links weaker than 1/300 of the strongest are dropped."},{"h":"A target curve in the map","p":"In the map, two points closer than min_dist should have similarity 1, and beyond that the similarity should fall as exp(−(d − min_dist)). That curve has a kink, so UMAP fits the smooth 1/(1 + a·d<sup>2b</sup>) to it. min_dist 0.1 gives a = 1.58, b = 0.90. min_dist 0.9 gives a = 0.16, b = 1.80."},{"h":"Pull and push","p":"Start from a rough layout of the graph (the spectral start). Then for 300 rounds: each link pulls its two ends together, strong links more often; and for each pull, five random points are pushed away. The step size falls from 1 to 0 over the run, so the picture freezes at the end."},{"h":"What n_neighbors changes","p":"Small: only the closest ties, so fine detail but fragile. With n_neighbors = 2 each point is tied only to its nearest neighbour, the graph shatters into about 97 islands and the map is dust. Large: many weak ties across class boundaries, so groups blur together and sit closer."},{"h":"What min_dist changes","p":"Nothing in the graph. It changes only the pull and push in the last step. At 0 points are free to pile up into tight clumps, useful when the map feeds a clustering step. At 0.9 they must keep apart, which shows structure inside a group and makes a better picture."}],"example":{"title":"strengths of one point's links, found by hand","setup":"A point P has n_neighbors = 5, so four neighbours besides itself, at distances <b>1.0, 1.2, 1.5 and 2.5</b>. The target is that its strengths add up to log₂ 5 = 2.32.","table":{"head":["Neighbour","Distance d","d − ρ (ρ = 1.0)","Strength exp(−(d − ρ)/0.67)"],"rows":[["1st","1.0","0.0","1.00"],["2nd","1.2","0.2","0.74"],["3rd","1.5","0.5","0.47"],["4th","2.5","1.5","0.11"]]},"walk":["ρ is the nearest distance, 1.0. Subtract it: d − ρ = 0, 0.2, 0.5, 1.5. The first strength is exp(0) = 1 whatever σ is.","Try σ = 0.5: strengths 1, exp(−0.4) = 0.67, exp(−1.0) = 0.37, exp(−3.0) = 0.05. They add up to 2.09. Too small, so widen σ.","Try σ = 1.0: 1, 0.82, 0.61, 0.22. Sum 2.65. Too big.","Try σ = 0.7: 1, 0.75, 0.49, 0.12. Sum 2.36, a little high. At σ = 0.67 the sum is 2.32, the target. (UMAP finds this by halving a bracket, as t-SNE does.)","Strengths at σ = 0.67: <b>1.00, 0.74, 0.47, 0.11</b>. The farthest neighbour is tied at only 11% strength.","Now merge directions. Say P's 3rd neighbour Q gives P a strength of 0.30 in its own list. Then w = 0.47 + 0.30 − 0.47 × 0.30 = 0.47 + 0.30 − 0.14 = <b>0.63</b>, stronger than either direction alone."],"result":"Four links from P with strengths 1.00, 0.74, 0.47, 0.11 that add up to log₂ 5. In the simulation, links are drawn fainter the weaker they are, so the 0.11 link is nearly invisible and also pulls only about once in nine rounds. With n_neighbors = 15 the same recipe runs over 14 neighbours."},"read":[["Dropdown: Dataset","Digits (400 images, 10 classes), Swiss roll (400 points, colour is position along the sheet) or Customers (3 segments in 10 features)."],["UMAP map","The 2-D result, drawn as digit numbers, colour gradient or markers, depending on the dataset. Hover over a digit to see its class."],["Checkbox: Show the neighbour graph","Draws the links behind the points. Stronger links are darker, weak ones are almost invisible."],["Curve plot","Solid line: the smooth curve 1/(1 + a·dist<sup>2b</sup>) that UMAP uses. Dashed line: the target it approximates, 1 until min_dist, then exp(−(dist − min_dist)). The dotted vertical line marks min_dist."],["Readout: Curve parameters a, b","The two fitted numbers. They depend on min_dist only."],["Readout: Edges in the graph","Number of links, and links per point. This depends on n_neighbors only."],["Readouts: Trustworthiness and Neighbour purity","Quality of the finished map. Purity is the share of a point's 10 nearest map neighbours with the same class (digit or segment). It reads n/a for the Swiss roll, which has no classes."],["Readout: Edges between different classes (share of edges · share of strength)","Two shares for links joining points of different classes (digits or segments): first the share of all edges by number, with the count, then the share of the total edge strength. n/a for the Swiss roll."],["Readout: Fit time","Includes the animation, so it is not a speed benchmark."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Choose Digits and n_neighbors 15. Set <i>min_dist</i> to 0, note the readouts, then set it to 0.9.","see":"Curve parameters go from 1.933, 0.790 to 0.165, 1.803. <i>Edges in the graph</i> stays 3592 (18.0 per point), and trustworthiness stays at about 0.975 in both. The map changes from tight dots to spread-out blobs: a point's nearest other point is about 5 times closer, relative to the map width, at min_dist 0 than at 0.9.","why":"min_dist enters only in the layout step. For a clustering step later, use 0, so groups become dense and separate. For a picture people will read, use a larger value so dots do not pile up."},{"do":"Set <i>n_neighbors</i> to 3, note the readouts and the map, then set it to 60.","see":"At 3: 580 links (2.9 per point), 4.7% of them by number and 4.3% by strength between different digits, trustworthiness about 0.96, and many small clumps (each digit in several pieces). At 60: 14,247 links (71 per point), 59.7% by number and 27.6% by strength between different digits, trustworthiness about 0.97, bigger groups that sit closer together and partly overlap. Purity is about 0.88 to 0.89 in both, so it hardly differs.","why":"More neighbours means longer, weaker links that cross class boundaries. UMAP sees a broader picture and loses fine detail. Neither trustworthiness nor purity tells you which is right. They stay within 0.96 to 0.98 from 3 to 80."},{"do":"Set <i>n_neighbors</i> to 2.","see":"Edges fall to 303 (1.5 per point), trustworthiness collapses to about 0.7 and the map is a dust of tiny islands.","why":"With n_neighbors = 2 each point links only to its single nearest neighbour. The graph then breaks into about 97 separate pieces, and the layout step has nothing to hold the pieces in relation to each other."},{"do":"Back to n_neighbors 15. Tick <i>Show the neighbour graph</i> and read <i>Edges between different classes</i>.","see":"22.0% of the edges (791 of 3592) join different digits, but only 12.9% of the total strength, and they are drawn faint.","why":"Cross-class links are the weak ones, so counting them by number overstates their effect. Counting by strength is the fairer measure."},{"do":"Choose the Swiss roll and compare <i>n_neighbors</i> 5 with 40.","see":"At 5 the roll breaks into about a dozen short strands (trustworthiness about 0.99). At 40 the strands join into one long ribbon (also about 0.99).","why":"5 neighbours reach only about 2.8 away, too short to tie the pieces together. At 40 the links reach 7.7, past the 6.3 gap between layers. But strength fades with distance: 22% of the links then reach another turn, yet they carry only 5% of the total strength. The strong links stay on the sheet and the extra weaker ones join the strands."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Friends list and poster.</b> n_neighbors = how many friends each student lists. min_dist = how close the dots may be drawn on the poster."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"<b>n_neighbors is what you believe about the data. min_dist is how you draw it.</b> If the graph readouts (edges, cross-class share) did not move, you changed only the drawing."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Start at n_neighbors = 15 and min_dist = 0.1. Try 5 and 50 and keep what both show. For a clustering step use min_dist 0, for a picture 0.5 or more."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Nearest neighbour = strength 1. Others = exp(−(extra distance)/σ). Two directions merge as a + b − ab."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Links per point should be about n_neighbors to 1.2 times n_neighbors. If it is far below, or trustworthiness collapses (about 0.7 at n_neighbors = 2), the graph has shattered. Raise n_neighbors."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>UMAP(n_neighbors=15, min_dist=0.1, random_state=0)</code>. After <code>fit</code>, <code>.graph_</code> holds the links and <code>.embedding_</code> the map."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"min_dist changes which points count as neighbours.\"","right":"The graph is identical for min_dist 0 and 0.9 (same 3592 links). It changes only how tightly the layout packs points."},{"wrong":"\"A bigger n_neighbors is always safer.\"","right":"At 60 neighbours 59.7% of the links by number, and 27.6% by strength, join different digits (4.7% and 4.3% at 3 neighbours). Groups blur and sit closer. Bigger gives a broader view, not a better one."},{"wrong":"\"Pick the n_neighbors with the highest trustworthiness.\"","right":"For the digits it sits at 0.96 to 0.98 for every value from 3 to 80, so it cannot choose. Decide from what you need: fine detail or the big picture."},{"wrong":"\"The distance between dots in the map is the similarity UMAP found.\"","right":"Only the neighbour relations are used. How tightly points are packed was set by min_dist, not by the data."}],"code":[{"title":"Strengths of one point's links","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import brentq\n\nd = np.array([1.0, 1.2, 1.5, 2.5])            # distances to the 4 neighbours (n_neighbors = 5 counts the point itself)\nrho = d.min()                                  # nearest neighbour is always fully connected: strength 1\ntarget = np.log2(5)                            # strengths must add up to log2(n_neighbors) = 2.32\nmember = lambda s: np.exp(-(d - rho) / s)      # strength of each neighbour for a given sigma\nsigma = brentq(lambda s: member(s).sum() - target, 0.01, 100)    # find the sigma that hits the target\nprint(\"rho\", rho, \" sigma\", round(sigma, 2))\nprint(\"memberships\", np.round(member(sigma), 2), \" sum\", round(member(sigma).sum(), 2))\na, b = member(sigma)[2], 0.30                  # P's view of Q is 0.47; Q's view of P is 0.30\nprint(\"edge weight a + b - ab =\", round(a + b - a * b, 2))","out":"rho 1.0  sigma 0.67\nmemberships [1.   0.74 0.47 0.11]  sum 2.32\nedge weight a + b - ab = 0.63","walk":[["rho = d.min()","The smallest distance, 1.0. Every strength uses <code>d - rho</code>, so the nearest neighbour gets exp(0) = 1."],["np.log2(5)","The target total, 2.32. Using log₂ of n_neighbors makes the total grow slowly with the neighbourhood size."],["member = lambda s: ...","A one-line function. Give it a σ and it returns the four strengths. The whole array is computed at once."],["brentq(f, 0.01, 100)","Finds the σ between 0.01 and 100 where <code>f</code> crosses zero, here where the sum of strengths equals the target. It does the halving search for us."],["member(sigma)[2]","Index 2 is the third neighbour (counting from 0): strength 0.47."],["a + b - a * b","The fuzzy union. It equals 1 − (1 − a)(1 − b): the chance that at least one of the two directions holds."]],"tip":"The same code with distances 2.0, 2.4, 3.0, 5.0 (everything doubled) gives the same strengths, because σ doubles too."},{"title":"min_dist changes the curve, not the graph","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom umap.umap_ import find_ab_params\n\ndist = np.array([0.2, 1.0, 2.0])               # three distances between two points in the map\nfor md in (0.1, 0.9):\n    a, b = find_ab_params(spread=1.0, min_dist=md)      # fit 1 / (1 + a * d^(2b)) to the target curve\n    print(\"min_dist\", md, \"-> a =\", round(a, 2), \"b =\", round(b, 2), \"similarity:\", np.round(1 / (1 + a * dist ** (2 * b)), 2))","out":"min_dist 0.1 -> a = 1.58 b = 0.9 similarity: [0.92 0.39 0.15]\nmin_dist 0.9 -> a = 0.16 b = 1.8 similarity: [1.   0.86 0.33]","walk":[["from umap.umap_ import find_ab_params","The helper UMAP itself uses to turn min_dist into <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>. It is in the package's inner module, hence the doubled name."],["find_ab_params(spread=1.0, min_dist=md)","Fits the smooth curve to the target. <code>spread=1.0</code> is the default width of the fall-off."],["dist ** (2 * b)","Each distance raised to the power 2b, elementwise. This is the d<sup>2b</sup> in the formula."],["1 / (1 + a * ...)","The similarity the layout aims for between two points a given distance apart: near 1 means \"attract\", near 0 means \"free to separate\"."]],"tip":"Read the second row: with min_dist 0.9 points at distance 1.0 still have similarity 0.86, so they are barely pulled together and the map stays spread out."},{"title":"Same graph, tighter packing","py":"import numpy as np, umap\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\nX = load_digits().data[:400]\nfits = {md: umap.UMAP(n_neighbors=15, min_dist=md, random_state=0).fit(X) for md in (0.0, 0.9)}\nprint(\"same neighbour graph:\", (fits[0.0].graph_ != fits[0.9].graph_).nnz == 0)\n\ndef packing(E):                                # typical gap to the nearest other point, relative to map width\n    gap = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=2).fit(E).kneighbors(E)[0][:, 1]\n    return np.median(gap) / np.ptp(E, axis=0).max()\nprint(\"points packed tighter at min_dist 0:\", packing(fits[0.0].embedding_) < packing(fits[0.9].embedding_))","out":"same neighbour graph: True\npoints packed tighter at min_dist 0: True","walk":[["{md: umap.UMAP(...).fit(X) for md in (0.0, 0.9)}","A dictionary comprehension: one fitted UMAP per min_dist, stored under that value as its key. Everything else is identical, including <code>random_state</code>."],["fits[0.0].graph_","The neighbour graph as a sparse matrix of link strengths (the trailing underscore marks a result of <code>fit</code>)."],["(g1 != g2).nnz == 0","Compare the two sparse matrices. <code>nnz</code> counts the entries that differ. Zero means the graphs are identical."],["NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=2)","Asks for each point's two nearest points in the map: itself and the nearest other point. Column <code>[:, 1]</code> keeps the distance to the nearest other one."],["np.ptp(E, axis=0).max()","The map's width: <code>ptp</code> is \"peak to peak\" (max minus min) in each coordinate, and <code>.max()</code> takes the wider one. Dividing by it makes maps of different size comparable."]],"tip":"The first line is the point of this lesson: the two maps differ, the graph behind them does not."}],"check":[{"q":"Which setting changes the neighbour graph that UMAP builds?","options":["n_neighbors only","min_dist only","Both of them","Neither, the graph depends only on the data"],"answer":0,"why":"The graph is built from the original data and n_neighbors before any map exists. min_dist enters later, in the layout step, so graphs for min_dist 0 and 0.9 are identical."},{"q":"Point P lists neighbour Q with strength 0.47, and Q lists P with strength 0.30. What is the merged link weight a + b − ab?","options":["0.63","0.38","0.14","0.77"],"answer":0,"why":"0.47 + 0.30 − 0.47 × 0.30 = 0.77 − 0.141 = 0.63. The merged weight is larger than either direction alone."},{"q":"You set n_neighbors = 2 on the 400 digits. What do you expect to see?","options":["A cleaner map with sharper clusters","A dust of tiny islands, and trustworthiness collapsing to about 0.7","One big blob of all digits","The same map as for n_neighbors = 15"],"answer":1,"why":"With 2 neighbours (the point itself and one more) each point links only to its nearest neighbour, so the graph falls into about 97 pieces. The layout cannot place the pieces sensibly and neighbours are wrong (trustworthiness about 0.7)."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend what n_neighbors and min_dist each control in UMAP.","model":"n_neighbors decides how many nearest points each point is linked to when UMAP builds its neighbour graph, so small values keep fine local detail and large values give a broader, blurrier view. min_dist does not touch the graph at all: it only sets how tightly the layout may pack points, so 0 gives dense clumps and a large value gives a spread-out picture."},"cheat":"UMAP builds a graph: each point links to its n_neighbors nearest, nearest = strength 1, others exp(−(d − ρ)/σ) with σ chosen so strengths add to log₂(n_neighbors), two directions merged as a + b − ab. n_neighbors alone sets this graph. min_dist only sets how tightly the layout packs points (curve parameters a and b). Small n_neighbors: detail, fragments. Large: broad, blurred."},"embeddings/new-points":{"idea":"UMAP learns a rule it can reuse: a new point is placed by looking up its nearest training points. t-SNE only places the points it was fitted on, so to add points you must refit, and the old points move too.","why":"A map is useful only if you can place tomorrow's customers on it, and a classifier that uses the two map coordinates must be tested on points the map never saw. With t-SNE the map changes whenever the data does, so last month's picture cannot be reused and your test score is not honest.","analogy":"A printed city map with a street index. A new house is placed by its nearest known neighbours, \"between 12 Park Road and the temple\", and the map stays as printed. t-SNE is a map that must be redrawn from scratch for every new house, and the redrawn map comes out rotated and rearranged, so last year's copy no longer matches.","vocab":[["Out-of-sample point","A point that was not used to fit the map: a new customer, or a held-out test digit."],["fit and transform","<code>fit</code> learns the map from the training data. <code>transform</code> places new points on that learned map without changing it. UMAP has both. scikit-learn's TSNE has no <code>transform</code>."],["Training and test set","Here 300 digits (30 per class) fit the map and 100 digits (10 per class) are held out to test it."],["10-NN accuracy","Classify a point by a vote of its 10 nearest training points, in the map or in the original pixels, and count the share it gets right."],["Baseline","The same classifier on the original 64 pixels. A map is worth using if its accuracy comes close to this with only 2 numbers per point."],["Data leakage","Information from the test rows reaching the training step, which makes the test score look better than it will be on genuinely new data."],["Refit","Running t-SNE again on training plus new points together. Every point, old and new, is free to move."]],"steps":[{"h":"A t-SNE map is a list, not a rule","p":"After t-SNE finishes you hold a table: one (x, y) row per point it was fitted on. Nothing in it says how to turn 64 pixel values into (x, y). A new image has no place on the map until you run the whole optimisation again."},{"h":"UMAP keeps what it needs","p":"After fitting, UMAP keeps the training data and the map positions it found. That is enough to define a rule for new points: put the new point where the training points that resemble it sit."},{"h":"Neighbours and strengths","p":"For a new digit, find its n_neighbors nearest training digits by pixel distance (not by map distance). Give them strengths with the same recipe as in the fit: ρ is the nearest distance, the nearest gets 1, and σ is searched so that the strengths add up to log₂(n_neighbors)."},{"h":"Start at a weighted average","p":"Place the new point at the strength-weighted average of its neighbours' map positions. Heavy neighbours pull most. This start is already close to the right place."},{"h":"Polish with the training points frozen","p":"For 100 short rounds the new point is pulled toward its neighbours and pushed from random training points, as in the fit. Only the new point moves. The training points stay exactly where they were, so the map you already show people is unchanged."},{"h":"Use the coordinates as features","p":"Train a classifier on the 300 training map positions and score it on the 100 placed test points. In the simulation, 10-NN on 2 UMAP numbers gets about 94% of the test digits right (92 to 96% over seeds), against 95% for 10-NN on all 64 pixels."},{"h":"t-SNE can only refit","p":"To place the 100 test digits with t-SNE you fit again on all 400. The 300 old points are free to move. After the best possible rotation or flip, the median old point still moves 13 to 14 units, about a quarter (24 to 28%) of the map width. A picture you showed last week no longer matches."},{"h":"Fit inside the training fold","p":"If you fit UMAP on all rows and then split into train and test, the test rows have helped shape the map, so the score is a little optimistic: 0.99 against 0.96 in our run. UMAP never sees the labels, so this is a milder leak than using y, but it is still a leak. Fit on the training rows only."}],"example":{"title":"placing one new point by hand","setup":"A new digit has its four nearest training digits at pixel distances <b>2.0, 2.5, 3.5 and 6.0</b>. Their positions on the map are in the last column. Where does UMAP first put the new point? (Here n_neighbors = 4, so the strengths must add up to log₂ 4 = 2.)","table":{"head":["Training neighbour","Distance d","d − ρ (ρ = 2.0)","Strength (σ = 1.23)","Share","Map position"],"rows":[["A","2.0","0.0","1.000","0.500","(2.0, 1.0)"],["B","2.5","0.5","0.666","0.333","(3.0, 1.5)"],["C","3.5","1.5","0.295","0.148","(2.5, 0.0)"],["D","6.0","4.0","0.039","0.019","(6.0, 3.0)"]]},"walk":["ρ is the nearest distance, 2.0. So d − ρ = 0, 0.5, 1.5, 4.0 and the first strength is exp(0) = 1.","Find σ so that the strengths add up to 2. Try σ = 1.0: 1 + 0.607 + 0.223 + 0.018 = 1.848, too small. Try σ = 1.3: 1 + 0.681 + 0.315 + 0.046 = 2.042, too big. Try σ = 1.2: 1.981. At σ = 1.23 the sum is 2.000.","Strengths at σ = 1.23: exp(−0.5/1.23) = 0.666, exp(−1.5/1.23) = 0.295, exp(−4.0/1.23) = 0.039, and 1 for A.","Turn them into shares by dividing by their sum (2.000): 0.500, 0.333, 0.148, 0.019.","Weighted average of the map positions: x = 0.500 × 2.0 + 0.333 × 3.0 + 0.148 × 2.5 + 0.019 × 6.0 = 1.000 + 0.999 + 0.369 + 0.116 = <b>2.48</b>.","y = 0.500 × 1.0 + 0.333 × 1.5 + 0.148 × 0.0 + 0.019 × 3.0 = 0.500 + 0.500 + 0 + 0.058 = <b>1.06</b>."],"result":"The new point starts at (2.48, 1.06), between A and B, close to the heaviest neighbour. D, six away in pixels, hardly tugs it. Then 100 small steps polish the position. A, B, C and D do not move."},"read":[["Left card: UMAP, fitted on 300 training digits, then test digits transformed","Faded digit numbers are the 300 training digits. Bold digit numbers inside a coloured ring are the 100 test digits placed by <code>transform</code>. A ring that sits among faded digits with the same number means a good placement."],["Right card: t-SNE, training digits only","Before you press the button: a t-SNE map of the 300 training digits. After refitting: faded numbers are the old positions, solid numbers the new positions of the same training digits, and bold ringed numbers the test digits as the refit placed them."],["Lines on the t-SNE card (arrows)","Each joins a training digit's old position to its position after the refit, once the best rotation or flip has been applied. Long lines mean the map was rearranged."],["Readout: Test accuracy, 10-NN on the 64 pixels","The baseline: what the same classifier scores on the original features."],["Readout: Test accuracy, 10-NN on UMAP coordinates","The score using only the two map coordinates of the transformed test points. The 10 neighbours are the training digits on the UMAP map."],["Readout: Training points moved by transform","Measured by reading the fitted positions again after <code>transform</code>. It always says \"none (0 of 300)\", because <code>transform</code> never changes the stored training positions."],["Readout: t-SNE: median move of a training point","After the refit: how far the typical training digit moved, in map units and as a share of the map width."],["Slider: UMAP n_neighbors","Neighbourhood size for both the fit and the transform."],["Checkboxes: Show training digits / Show transformed test digits (circled)","Hide one group to see the other on its own."],["Buttons: New seed and Refit t-SNE with the test points","<i>New seed</i> reruns UMAP with another random start. The t-SNE map starts from PCA, so its picture does not change. <i>Refit</i> becomes available when the t-SNE map of the training digits has finished."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Read <i>Test accuracy, 10-NN on the 64 pixels</i> and <i>Test accuracy, 10-NN on UMAP coordinates</i>. Press <i>New seed</i> twice more.","see":"The baseline is 95%. The UMAP readout is about 94% (between 93% and 96% in our runs, whatever the seed).","why":"Only 100 test digits, so one wrong digit costs a point. Two map coordinates keep almost all the class information that 64 pixel values carry, because digits of the same class sit close together on the map."},{"do":"Press <i>Refit t-SNE with the test points</i> and wait for the run to finish.","see":"Lines appear on the t-SNE card. The readout says the median training digit moved about 13 to 14 units, roughly a quarter (24 to 28%) of the map width. The UMAP readout still says \"none (0 of 300)\".","why":"The refit starts from the PCA of 400 digits and optimises everything again, so clusters shift and re-settle even after the best rotation. UMAP's transform never touched the training points."},{"do":"Untick <i>Show training digits</i>.","see":"Only the 100 ringed test digits remain. They form groups, one per digit, at the places where the training digits of the same number were.","why":"Each test digit was placed from its nearest training digits, so it lands among its own kind."},{"do":"Set <i>UMAP n_neighbors</i> to 5, then to 50.","see":"The accuracy hardly moves: in our runs it stayed between 92% and 96% at 5 and at 50 neighbours, and the lowest value we ever saw was 89% (at 50, in one run).","why":"With only 100 test digits one digit is one point, so differences of one or two points are noise. Fifty neighbours include more digits of other classes, as in the settings lesson, so a small loss is possible, but a few neighbours are enough to place a digit among its look-alikes."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"<b>Street index</b> against <b>redrawing the whole map</b>. UMAP looks the new house up. t-SNE draws a new map."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Anything whose output feeds a model must be <b>a function you can apply to new rows</b>: fit once, transform later. PCA and UMAP are. t-SNE is a picture, not a feature maker."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"After <code>transform</code>, the training embedding must be unchanged: <code>np.allclose(before, after)</code> is True. If it is not, you refitted by accident."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Always score the same classifier on the original features as a baseline. Here 95% on pixels against about 94% on 2 coordinates: the map lost almost nothing."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Fit the map on the training rows only, or put it in a <code>Pipeline</code> so that cross-validation refits it inside every fold."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"New position = strength-weighted average of the neighbours' map positions, then 100 small corrections."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"I can place test data with t-SNE by calling fit_transform on the test set.\"","right":"That fits a second, unrelated map with its own rotation and scale. Test coordinates and training coordinates then live in different frames, and a classifier trained on one is meaningless on the other."},{"wrong":"\"UMAP's transform moves the training points a little to make room.\"","right":"It does not. The stored map is untouched (the readout says 0 of 300). Only the new point is moved."},{"wrong":"\"Fitting the map on all the data is fine, because UMAP never sees the labels.\"","right":"It is milder than label leakage but it is still a leak: the test points shaped the neighbour graph. In our run the score was 0.99 against 0.96 when the map was fitted on training rows only."},{"wrong":"\"With t-SNE I just refit and the old points stay where they were.\"","right":"They do not. The median old point moves about a quarter of the map width (24 to 28% here), even after the best rotation or flip."}],"code":[{"title":"Fit UMAP on training digits, place unseen digits, classify","py":"import numpy as np, umap\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n\nX, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X[:1000], y[:1000], test_size=0.3, random_state=0)\n\num = umap.UMAP(n_neighbors=15, random_state=0).fit(X_tr)       # learn the map from training digits only\nE_tr = um.embedding_.copy()\nE_te = um.transform(X_te)                                       # place unseen digits on that map\nknn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=10).fit(E_tr, y_tr)\nprint(\"test shape:\", E_te.shape, \" accuracy above 0.9:\", knn.score(E_te, y_te) > 0.9)\nprint(\"training points moved:\", not np.allclose(E_tr, um.embedding_))","out":"test shape: (300, 2)  accuracy above 0.9: True\ntraining points moved: False","walk":[["train_test_split(X[:1000], y[:1000], test_size=0.3, random_state=0)","Split 1,000 digits into 700 training and 300 test rows. It returns four arrays in this fixed order: training features, test features, training labels, test labels. <code>random_state</code> fixes the shuffle."],["umap.UMAP(...).fit(X_tr)","<code>fit</code> learns the map from the 700 training digits only. The test digits have not been seen yet."],["um.embedding_.copy()","The learned training positions (underscore = result of <code>fit</code>). <code>.copy()</code> saves a snapshot so we can check later that nothing moved."],["um.transform(X_te)","Places the 300 test digits on the learned map. Shape (300, 2): one row per digit, two coordinates. This method does not exist on scikit-learn's TSNE."],["KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=10).fit(E_tr, y_tr)","A 10-NN classifier trained on the 2-D training positions and their labels. <code>score</code> on the test positions gives the share it gets right."],["np.allclose(E_tr, um.embedding_)","True if the training positions are unchanged by <code>transform</code>. We print <code>not</code> of it, so False means nothing moved."]],"tip":"We print True/False instead of the accuracy because the exact value shifts a little with library versions. In our run it was about 0.96."},{"title":"t-SNE has no transform; a refit moves the old points","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.manifold import TSNE\nfrom scipy.linalg import orthogonal_procrustes\n\nX = load_digits().data[:400]\nprint(\"TSNE has transform:\", hasattr(TSNE(), \"transform\"))\n\nold = TSNE(init=\"pca\", random_state=0).fit_transform(X[:300])                 # map of 300 training digits\nnew = TSNE(init=\"pca\", random_state=0).fit_transform(X)[:300]                 # refit with 100 more digits added\na, b = old - old.mean(0), new - new.mean(0)\nb = b @ orthogonal_procrustes(b, a)[0]                                        # remove rotation and flip first\nmove = np.linalg.norm(a - b, axis=1) / np.ptp(a, axis=0).max()                # displacement / map width\nprint(\"median move above 5% of the map width:\", np.median(move) > 0.05)","out":"TSNE has transform: False\nmedian move above 5% of the map width: True","walk":[["hasattr(TSNE(), \"transform\")","Asks Python whether the object has an attribute called <code>transform</code>. For TSNE the answer is False: it can only <code>fit_transform</code>."],["TSNE(...).fit_transform(X)[:300]","Refit on all 400 digits, then keep only the first 300 rows: the old training digits at their new positions."],["old - old.mean(0)","Subtract the average position so both maps are centred on the origin, a step needed before aligning."],["orthogonal_procrustes(b, a)[0]","The best rotation or flip matrix that takes <code>b</code> as close as possible to <code>a</code>. <code>b @ R</code> applies it. After this, any remaining difference is a real move, not a turn."],["np.linalg.norm(a - b, axis=1)","For each of the 300 digits, the length of the arrow from old to new position."],["np.ptp(a, axis=0).max()","The map's width, so the move can be given as a share of it. <code>np.median(...)</code> then takes the typical move."]],"tip":"The simulation's lines are exactly these arrows. We print a True/False threshold because the exact share depends on the library version."},{"title":"Why the map must be fitted on the training rows only","py":"import numpy as np, umap\nfrom sklearn.datasets import load_digits\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n\nX, y = load_digits(return_X_y=True)\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X[:1000], y[:1000], test_size=0.3, random_state=0)\nscore = lambda E_tr, E_te: KNeighborsClassifier(10).fit(E_tr, y_tr).score(E_te, y_te)\n\nhonest = umap.UMAP(random_state=0).fit(X_tr)                           # test digits never seen by the map\nE = umap.UMAP(random_state=0).fit_transform(np.vstack([X_tr, X_te]))   # test digits help shape the map\nleaky = score(E[:len(y_tr)], E[len(y_tr):])\nprint(\"map fitted on everything scores higher:\", leaky > score(honest.embedding_, honest.transform(X_te)))","out":"map fitted on everything scores higher: True","walk":[["score = lambda E_tr, E_te: ...","A short function that trains a 10-NN classifier on training map positions and returns the accuracy on test map positions. Written once, used twice."],["honest = umap.UMAP(...).fit(X_tr)","The correct way: the map is learned without the test digits, which are placed later with <code>transform</code>."],["np.vstack([X_tr, X_te])","Stack the two feature tables on top of each other: 1,000 rows. This map is fitted with the test rows included."],["E[:len(y_tr)]  /  E[len(y_tr):]","Split the joint map back into the first 700 rows (training) and the last 300 (test)."],["leaky > score(...)","The leaky score is higher because test points helped decide the neighbour graph, so they land among their own kind more reliably than a truly new point would."]],"tip":"The gap is small here (about 3 points in our run) but it is always in the flattering direction. Fit inside the training fold."}],"check":[{"q":"Which operation places a new customer on an existing 2-D map without changing the map?","options":["Refitting PCA on the new customers","UMAP's transform","Calling TSNE().fit_transform on the new customers alone","Calling TSNE().transform on the new customers"],"answer":1,"why":"UMAP keeps the training data and positions and looks the new point up among them. scikit-learn's TSNE has no transform. fit_transform on new rows would draw a second, unrelated map. Refitting PCA on the new rows would also change the map."},{"q":"A new point has two neighbours on the map at (0, 0) and (4, 0) with strengths 1.0 and 0.25. Where does UMAP start it?","options":["(3.2, 0)","(0.8, 0)","(2, 0)","(1, 0)"],"answer":1,"why":"The shares are 1.0/1.25 = 0.8 and 0.25/1.25 = 0.2. So x = 0.8 × 0 + 0.2 × 4 = 0.8, and y = 0."},{"q":"You fit UMAP on all 1,000 digits, then split into 700 training and 300 test rows and train a classifier on the map coordinates. Compared with fitting UMAP on the 700 training rows and transforming the test rows, the test score is likely to be:","options":["Impossible to compute","Identical, because UMAP never sees the labels","Lower, because the map has more points to fit","Slightly higher than the honest score, because the test rows helped shape the map"],"answer":3,"why":"The test rows were part of the neighbour graph, so they sit more cleanly among their own class than a new point would. The score is optimistic, even though labels were not used."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why UMAP can place new points on a map but t-SNE cannot, and what that means for testing a classifier that uses the map.","model":"UMAP keeps its training points and finds a new point's nearest training points in the original features, then places it at their weighted average position and polishes it without moving the old points. t-SNE only returns coordinates for the points it fitted, so adding points means a refit that moves everything, and a classifier built on map coordinates must be tested on points placed by a transform of a map fitted on the training rows only."},"cheat":"UMAP.transform places a new point by finding its nearest training points in the original space, giving them fuzzy strengths, starting at their weighted average map position and polishing for 100 rounds with the training points frozen. t-SNE has no transform: a refit moves old points (about a quarter of the map width here). Fit the map on training rows only, then transform the test rows."}});
